window.harnessChunks["rows-376dea1451735800.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-15T04:50:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pedapudi/foe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Foe</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-15\">2026-10-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pedapudi/foe/tree/167455955b79ee31e7e8ac3c2ecfff40a6b14ec2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 167455955b79ee31e7e8ac3c2ecfff40a6b14ec2\">1674559 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute one bounded code investigation/edit/test/report task through model-guided tool use and environmental feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different source tree/test observations, errors, model/tool outputs, tool denials, returned messages and task-specific constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what source/tool/action to inspect or modify next and when to report a solution, blockage or need for assistance. - Decision owner: the model-driven episode actor, inside the first-party Foe autonomous run loop; externally hosted inference computes responses but does not supply a separate harness&#39;s execution/control organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rust tool registry and coding functions, sandbox/grants, logged model turns, budget, completion verifier and typed outcome. - Closure path: objective/inbox → model-request → proposed tool call → first-party validated dispatch → result recorded in log → next model observation/action or terminal verified outcome. - Boundary reachability: standard foe executable with built-in coding/oneshot/team contracts launches this first-party loop without borrowing an external coding-agent CLI, eval fixture or user-authored work scheduler. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model chooses task-specific actions and reacts to feedback; deterministic constraints veto unsafe/over-budget work but cannot choose the semantic next solution step by themselves. - Evidence: README.md; crates/core/src/loop.rs; crates/cli/src/run.rs; crates/code/src/lib.rs; docs/design.md. - Basis: explicit shipped wiring and code. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a deliberately model-less deterministic workflow alone would not justify S1=A; positive evidence is actual model/tool episode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate work interference and oscillation between distinct concurrently operating child episode S1s in a team, especially contested mutable code paths and task dependency cycles/overlap. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two S1s trying to modify overlapping filesystem scopes, stale dependent work launched too early, messages/questions that would induce duplicate or conflicting efforts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select independent/non-overlapping work partitions, keep shared surfaces with the lead, order dependent units, and revise subordinate work when child results or risks change. This is a specific model-directed coordination judgment; the kernel&#39;s overlap refusal is the enforcement layer. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven team lead exercising spawn, blockedby, send, steer, cancel and wait in the shipped builtin:team contract; the deterministic board denies invalid overlapping roots and prevents concurrent writers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: canonical path overlap check on board admission and at child launch, dependency DAG, unique task assignment, single-writer log projection, per-child grants/sandbox, peer-message delivery with reply ids/deadlines. - Closure path: leader partitions tasks → Team::delegate rejects an overlapping current write root unless dependent → scheduler admits disjoint ready children with restricted grants → child outcomes and notifications return through durable team log/board/inbox → leader&#39;s next model turn revises/continues partition, or reorders/cancels affected work. - Boundary reachability: builtin:team provides this rule explicitly and provides model-callable coordination tools; separately launched child processes, scoped grants and board are part of the shipped CLI/runtime distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the lead&#39;s task-specific choice of conflict-free partition and follow-up from actual worker feedback is not determined by the static scheduler. Removing the lead&#39;s model leaves mechanical collision rejection/dependency order but removes the actual substantive team-level response to coding conflicts; this distinguishes owner from enforcer. - Distinct S1 units: separately running worker and surveyor episodes with their own model/tool loops or read-only task outcome, in the root team; recursive children may add subordinate teams. - Inter-S1 disturbance: potential simultaneous writes under the same source directory, incompatible edits to shared interfaces, dependent changes proceeding before prerequisites and worker waiting/communication stalling. - Attenuating coordination relation: leader-authored partition/ownership rules, immutable in-boundary child grants, overlap-refusal and dependency gating, bounded reply correlation and cancellation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: board/task revisions and child outcome/inbox evidence influence leader&#39;s next spawn/steer/cancel/integration decisions, and those decisions return to affected child tasks. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: concurrent autonomous child S1s share writeable source resources, the code detects specific overlapping write conflict before launch, and leader-owned adaptive partition/replanning responds with enforced new S1 boundaries. This is concrete interference attenuation beyond mere channels or task-order fields. - Evidence: crates/cli/src/builtin-team.json; crates/team/src/lib.rs; crates/team/src/libtest.rs; docs/design.md; crates/core/src/spawn.rs. - Basis: explicit causal code, supported built-in mode and structural owner test. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A is limited to a model-driven lead in builtin team mode; kernel-only overlap enforcement is deterministic and would not separately be S2=A. Local quality/correctness of final merged code is not guaranteed by partitioning.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate whole-current commitments, bounded resources, team membership and intervention at the declared single-root-team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: insufficient child capacity or token/time budget, blocked dependencies, failed/cancelled workers, incomplete/unaccounted deliverables, unnecessary or wrong delegated units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: from a team-wide board/roster and returned worker outcomes, decide which unit to perform internally, which child to spawn and with what scope, which child to steer or cancel, and how/when to integrate accounted work or exit. - Decision owner: the model-driven lead episode in builtin:team. Team::schedule, budget pool, verification and log are bounded machinery executing decisions rather than owning team-level discretionary work/commitment allocation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task board with queued/running/terminal revisions and owner/dependencies, roster, shared child budget reservations and releases, team/wait tools, bounded completion accounting, stop and recovery. - Closure path: leader reads the current team/task/worker state → chooses spawn/steer/cancel/wait/reallocation/integration → admission, execution and status changes are journaled and enforced → next model turn sees settlement/progress and can revise current commitments. - Boundary reachability: packaged built-in team lead and team tools operate in the normal binary without a separate coordinator service, external human dispatcher or development-only example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the lead chooses which work and constraints to commit in light of current whole-team state; deterministic budget rules, task statuses, and verifier account checks only transport and enforce those choices. - Whole-system current view: team-wide log-projected task board (task id, owner, status, dependencies, write scopes, outcomes), roster, messages, and shared descendant budget accounting at this team&#39;s recursion, via first-party team, wait, and task-result surfaces. - Current-control decision scope: delegation, prioritization of current units, disjoint grants, current resource reservations (within declared ceilings), intervention/cancellation, task accounting and final integrated commitments; not ultimate organization-wide policy. - Evidence: crates/cli/src/builtin-team.json; crates/team/src/lib.rs; crates/core/src/loop.rs; crates/core/src/budget.rs; docs/design.md. - Basis: explicit shipped loop and agent-decided team regulation. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: S3 belongs to this root team&#39;s recursion, not Foe-wide stewardship across different user installations. No positive parent-governed variant inferred from an operator providing the initial task or inspecting the viewer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no complementary independent organizational inspection and corrective-return relationship distinct from the normal root lead/episode feedback is established in supported runtime modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect coding changes, verifier errors, missing task accounting or unsafe execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a complementary actor would independently inspect direct evidence about current operation, challenge ordinary S3 reports, form an audit finding and cause a corrective decision/return. - Decision owner: the configured verifier/tool produces determinate acceptance/findings as part of ordinary execution; team lead accounts for units and responds to returned findings; no separate independent S3 corrective authority is shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: donewhen.verify and verifier result events, append-only logs, final outcome evidence, lead&#39;s wait/task accountability, sandbox/grants and optional viewer/benchmark reports. - Closure path: verifier reports can trigger same episode&#39;s retry or failure; child outcome reports can trigger lead response. No separate independently owned complementary audit path into S3 is demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model tool feedback or externally authored deterministic verifier is ordinary quality control, and read-only surveyors are task contributors, not guaranteed independent evidence auditors with corrective return rights. - Evidence: crates/core/src/loop.rs; crates/cli/src/builtin-team.json; docs/evidence.md; docs/design.md; docs/evaluation.md. - Basis: bounded source-negative mapping. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a particular configured independent code audit episode can be constructed; no mandatory first-party S3 arrangement or dedicated audit correction path in standard modes is evidenced.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped prospective environmental/capability adaptation loop returning from external future intelligence to the operational team&#39;s capability baseline. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolving coding ecosystem, new threats/provider interfaces, accumulated failures and hypothetical future runtime improvements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently select future capability/strategy changes based on outside/forward-looking evidence and return adaptation to currently executing coding organization. - Decision owner: human operators/maintainers choose upgrades and configuration; local model may propose coding changes as its task, but the standard runtime does not own autonomous future organizational adaptation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic coding/edit tools, optional task-specific research, log history, candidate workflows and trajectory evidence, manually selected self-improvement workflows. - Closure path: examples/self-extension and evals/terminalbench can assess evidence and suggest edits in isolated evaluation tasks, but are adjacent eval/demo workflows and do not automatically promote a runtime/capability change into ordinary deployed Foe operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: changing some code under a coding task is ordinary S1; neither task-specific model planning nor append-only learning artifacts autonomously chooses future whole-harness adaptation from external environment. - Evidence: docs/self-improvement.md; docs/deferred.md; docs/design.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit documented limitation and bounded absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Foe&#39;s research reports describe bounded source-edit loops but explicitly say autonomous selection/promotion/general self-improvement remain unsupported; no inference that S4 is impossible to build atop its runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: the runtime enforces author-defined operational grants and contract values but no independently closed ultimate identity/policy governance function exists at selected team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsafe tools, write authority, budget and task scope, blocked questions and possible policy conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide or legitimately escalate ultimate purpose/identity conflicts beyond current work, return binding resolved policy to ongoing operations. - Decision owner: human/operator authors outer contract and permissions; children are granted only narrower scoped contracts. Model lead cannot legitimately override the organization&#39;s ultimate source of authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: immutable contract fingerprints, grants and path overlap, permission/sandbox checks, role-defined child contracts, typed completion outcomes, agent ask and block. - Closure path: pre-authored rule → kernel enforcement. Bounded question/answer and ordinary task routing can return an operational answer, but no first-party ultimate identity-level dispute → legitimate authoritative adjudication → new organizational policy → subsequent governed operations path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an immutable access boundary or model choosing to self-block is operational safety, not S5 ultimate values; operator ability to launch/update an episode is not an S5 parent-governance mode. - Evidence: crates/contract/src/document.rs; crates/core/src/grants.rs; crates/team/src/lib.rs; crates/cli/src/builtin-team.json; docs/design.md. - Basis: source-bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: individual host institutions could implement identity/policy governance using the host protocol, but this is separate from the frozen standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5928c8889e1b4da97bb7f7c571c3b63c28758eec/assessments/foe.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5928c8889e1b4da97bb7f7c571c3b63c28758eec\">5928c88 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T00:23:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/context-labs/whip\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WhipCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/context-labs/whip/tree/801b3a4f9412356cf36934701bce67a1fe30f6bb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 801b3a4f9412356cf36934701bce67a1fe30f6bb\">801b3a4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: independently choose, execute, inspect and revise substantive coding and repository actions in a bounded session or delegated child task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code, instructions, tool errors, model responses, failed host operations, rejected permissions, children and new user messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root/child model selects rlmexec cells, file/shell/agent calls, retry or alternative action from observed host output. - Decision owner: configured inference model in each first-party AgentSession; fixed host modules admit and enforce but do not choose the coding solution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSession.RunTurn, single rlmexec tool, isolated kernels, durable transcript/context handles, typed host modules, scoped permissions and goal-loop support. - Closure path: coding request/current context → model-chosen host operation → file/tool result, failure or denial → subsequent model operation or returned completion. - Boundary reachability: built-in Coding() is the default shipped definition; daemon binding gives both normal roots and spawned child sessions this model/tool loop, and production modes route through the same recursive runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: substantive operations vary with inference decisions, rather than being entirely predetermined by a script. - Evidence: agent-loop documentation, coding definition, agent session. - Basis: frozen production-source tracing and documented shipped operating model. - Confidence: high for operative construction, not live model performance. - Caveats: no model/provider endpoint or live Whip agent was run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate cross-child interference over shared work and coordinate changes to delegated task boundaries while children make independent operating decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two workers attempting incompatible shared-state writes, concurrent file edits, stale progress updates, overlapping responsibilities and changed dependent task results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the parent model chooses which independently deciding child tackles which files/tasks, what capabilities are delegated, and how to react to returned failures or shared blackboard conflict; children can reread a rejected CAS and revise their own update. - Decision owner: root or intermediate parent model for task partition/repartition; each child model for retry after observed conflict; daemon/SQLite owns atomic enforcement only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agents.spawn/submit, one-hop durable mailbox, blackboard CAS/version and subscriptions, file mutation coordination locks, child budgets and delegated authority. - Closure path: concurrent child work reveals overlap, stale version or result dependency → shared-state CAS rejects a stale update and mail/subscriptions expose changed state → model rereads and can change its update, ownership partition or child instructions → revised S1 action. - Boundary reachability: Coding() installs agents, messages, state and file operations for real root and child kernels; shared-state CAS and subscriptions are wired into the daemon host. Direct-child messages and parent follow-up invoke retained production agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: atomic CAS and same-path locking alone would only be deterministic support; here an actual model has task-partition authority and can use failure/version feedback to alter subsequent independent child work. This is bounded to conflicts visible through the supplied coordination surfaces, not automatic semantic merging of arbitrary shell edits. - Evidence: concurrency ownership, host tools and CAS contract, shared-state store, boundary regression. - Basis: runtime source, shipped guidance and deterministic regression source inspection. - Confidence: medium-high for an optional model-owned conflict regulation path. - Caveats: the parent must actually choose suitable decomposition/reconciliation; the runtime does not independently make semantic cross-file conflict judgments. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent independently model-driven retained child AgentSession workers, with local prompts/transcripts and side-effect capabilities. - Inter-S1 disturbance: incompatible updates to blackboard/work files or reassignment of work after a sibling reports a result. - Attenuating coordination relation: atomic CAS and file locks refuse stale/shared mutations, while parent model-directed task partition and follow-up steering change who works on what. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: CAS error includes the current version; state subscriptions deliver updates; child completion/failure and custom messages wake the parent, which can steer children or change subsequent delegation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: two simultaneous operational owners and a concrete stale-write/overlapping-task disturbance are identified; CAS refusal and subsequent agent-owned repartition/retry regulate that interference. Mere mail delivery is not credited independently.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: supervise current commitments and constraints of the active root/child organization, selecting targeted intervention while work is already in progress. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stuck or unproductive children, resource oversubscription, wrong delegation, changing user priority and current permission exposure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: parent model chooses from agents.list/inspect results whether to submit revised work, stop/delete a subtree, change follow-on delegation or narrow capability/budget; in a separate human-client mode the operator can inspect the entire agent tree and cancel/stop/revoke. - Decision owner: the parent inference model in autonomous mode; the session&#39;s legitimate human operator in client-governed mode. Daemon actors only serialize and enforce the selected change. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current agent statuses, budget accounting, agents.submit/stop/delete, subtree terminalization, shared notices; inspector tree, active-turn cancellation, grant revocation and protocol controls. - Closure path: currently observed child state/message/budget → model or human decision to steer, stop, cancel or revoke → daemon admits an authorized control → active/queued child work or effective authority changes → further state is observable. - Boundary reachability: Coding() installs current-control agents tools and production runtime implements list/inspect/submit/stop; the shipped packages/app inspector maps live snapshot permissions to concrete stop/cancel/revoke commands. Both modes are connected to the same session store, not test-only controllers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-decided supervision is a higher current-cohort regulation loop beyond assigning one new task; the operator mode is legitimately parent-owned and separately closed. Fixed worker admission, kernel pools and quota checks alone are not the S3 owner. - Evidence: recursive runtime controls, protocol client control, agent inspector, budget and grant inspector. - Basis: source-traced model and first-party human-client ownership modes. - Confidence: high for current-control rights and return; actual quality of prioritization unmeasured. - Caveats: a root model can inspect/control its direct children, not bypass ancestry to arbitrary grandchild transcripts; every recursion is governed by its immediate parent. Operator intervention requires a connected authorized client. - Whole-system current view: model sees the active direct-child set and per-child status/budgets/messages at its recursion; operator tree view shows all retained descendants and active turns with effective controls. - Current-control decision scope: selective stop/delete, midturn steer, new queued input, child task redistribution, capability revocation and budget/permission constraints rather than only mechanically scheduled execution.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no installed first-party complementary audit function is established for claims or actual consequences of normal coding work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported success or correctness claims could require a reviewer; the frozen runtime provides generic tools to create one, not a material audit-specific owner/return by default. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an ad hoc spawned child can be asked to review, but no first-party auditor-specific mode, independent inspection requirement, verdict contract or application of findings is packaged. - Decision owner: an external user/workflow designer would have to supply an actual auditor prompt, evidence scope and follow-up policy; Coding() and the daemon supply only general-purpose model/task primitives. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional agents.spawn with narrowed read/shell capabilities, models.batch independent completions, messages, transcripts, context handles, tests and eval verifier. - Closure path: a user-composed reviewer might inspect files and send a message, but neither a first-party claim-relative independent judgment nor the findings-to-correction circuit is bound as a supported audit-specific runtime path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple independent model calls and a child literally named reviewer are insufficient without function-specific audit ownership and complementary operational evidence access. This is a bounded no-path result, not an impossibility of composing a review externally. - Evidence: generic reviewer example, built-in coding definition, named reviewer test fixture, evaluation workflow. - Basis: first-party definition, actual exposed modules, documented composition and adjacent evaluation boundaries. - Confidence: medium for scoped negative; generic composability should not be confused with a first-party S3 constructor. - Caveats: a user-installed audit-specific custom definition could change another deployment&#39;s assessed boundary, but it is not in the frozen standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party future-facing external sensing, adaptation option judgment and return into operational capability was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new providers, MCP tools, coding conventions and later tasks could require future adaptation, but inspected mechanisms process current configuration and tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model can inspect existing catalogued skills/tools and create present-task schedules; users can import existing skills/MCP configuration. No first-party prospective external-capability selection and closed adoption cycle was observed. - Decision owner: operator for available skill/provider integration and user instructions; task model for current coding choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: schedules module, project/standing instructions and skills discovery, MCP selection/import, persistent me.md notes, context compaction, model/provider choice, goals. - Closure path: current user/project configuration is loaded or a future timed task is queued; no evidenced outside/future options → evaluation → capability-change decision → return to present S1/S3 ability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: remembering task facts and loading existing instructions are not the strategic prospective adaptation function. - Evidence: runtime environment assembly, feature and skills contract, memory, coding definition. - Basis: installed-module and supported-configuration review. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded negative. - Caveats: users may design and install specialized future-scanning agents; this is external composition, not credited first-party closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no internal or formally returned parent decision loop over ultimate organizational identity, mission and governing policy is established within an installed coding session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operations can raise safety/permission conflicts, but the handled issues concern authorizing particular tools and resources, not adjudicating the organization&#39;s ultimate purpose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator grants/denies current tool actions or revokes child authority; developers set fixed default policies; no first-party ultimate-identity dispute process and binding decision return is exposed. - Decision owner: user/developer for current permissions, project goals and configuration, without a separately instantiated S5 governance function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: recursive permission grants, capability issuer chains, modes, user.ask, kernel limits, provenance, instruction hierarchy, audit trail and operator control. - Closure path: requested side effect → permission grant/denial → operation permitted or blocked; this is tactical authorization, not identity/policy dispute → legitimate final authority → returned constitutional ruling. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: strong deterministic boundaries and the human&#39;s ownership of a requested task do not automatically create ultimate organization-level S5. - Evidence: permission and authority runtime, concurrency/authority, client policy/control, definition/identity. - Basis: first-party trust boundary and control-plane source review. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded negative. - Caveats: a human can change the task and software settings, but this does not evidence a complete S5=P loop at the session recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cb2b50d4a237e6757b5e5e1875aac5069febe9d2/assessments/wh/whipcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cb2b50d4a237e6757b5e5e1875aac5069febe9d2\">cb2b50d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T09:34:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/wkbin/taixu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TaiXu</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/wkbin/taixu/tree/037880a898ad503128dee7b2991dd6d351ad8cb3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 037880a898ad503128dee7b2991dd6d351ad8cb3\">037880a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-directed work through persistent model/tool execution, including workspace, shell, browser/MCP and other first-party tools, and return tool observations to the model until the work concludes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, changing workspace state, tool outputs/failures, schema errors, loop conditions, external resources and task-specific follow-up needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which available tool to call, with which task-specific arguments, and whether to continue using tools or conclude. - Decision owner: the configured main-session model actor; bounded child lanes independently own the same kind of local operational discretion for their assigned task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HarnessLoop, provider protocol, HarnessToolRoundRunner, ToolExecutor, schema/loop checks, persisted session/lane transcript, tool budgets and approval gates. - Closure path: user request/history → model chooses tool call → host validates/executes → ToolResult is persisted into the conversation → later model round observes the result and chooses the next operational action or final answer. - Boundary reachability: this is TaiXu&#39;s standard shipped chat/agent runtime; subagent lanes use the same first-party provider/tool protocol inside the packaged distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model removes the task-specific choice of tools, arguments and stopping point; deterministic machinery constrains and executes those choices but does not replace their ordinary operational discretion. - Evidence: HarnessToolRoundRunner.kt, SubagentLaneRunner.kt, HarnessLoop.kt. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent destructive interference among concurrently executing subagent S1 units that share a workspace, especially overlapping writes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two or more child lanes can otherwise concurrently modify the same file/directory or whole workspace, causing write collisions and invalidating each other&#39;s operational results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the task-specific write lease for each delegated S1 ([], exact paths/directories, or []) and thereby determine which child operations may safely coexist. - Decision owner: the main-session model actor issuing invokesubagent; first-party prompt/schema makes writePaths part of the model&#39;s required task decomposition/coordination decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: buildWriteCleanWaves, global parallelism gate, per-lane execution-time write-scope rejection, normalized path checks, blocked-write accounting and structured batch summaries. - Closure path: main model defines multiple child tasks and write leases → orchestrator detects lease overlap and partitions work into non-conflicting waves → lane gate blocks any structured write outside the selected lease and returns the failure as a tool result → the child changes later behavior, while later conflicting waves wait → parent receives combined outcomes for further coordination. - Boundary reachability: invokesubagent is a first-party model-facing tool in the ordinary system prompt/runtime; the shipped guidance explicitly requires writePaths, and SubagentOrchestrator/SubagentLaneRunner enforce the same fields without downstream code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the host decides deterministic overlap/scheduling from the chosen leases, but the task-specific coordination right — what each child may write and whether it is read-only/scoped/exclusive — belongs to the main agent. Without that model decision the host does not infer materially equivalent task-specific leases. - Evidence: subagentguidance.md, SubagentArgsParser.kt, SubagentOrchestrator.kt, SubagentLaneContracts.kt, SubagentLaneRunner.kt. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the lease gate does not cover arbitrary shell writes through base; the shipped prompt states this boundary and directs strict-isolation writes through structured write/edit tools. - Distinct S1 units: separately named/resumable subagent lanes, each running its own bounded model/tool loop with its own operation/transcript while contributing to the parent job. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent lane writes to overlapping workspace paths can collide or invalidate peer work. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-declared write leases plus deterministic conflict partitioning into write-clean waves and execution-time path enforcement. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: out-of-lease writes are returned to the offending child as failed tool results; conflicting tasks are delayed to later waves; blocked/incomplete outcomes are returned to the parent for repair rather than silently treated as complete. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is explicitly designed around the concrete shared-workspace write-interference mode and changes when/how affected child operations may write.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current set of child operations as a whole, allocate their task/model/write commitments, and intervene when a child is incomplete, blocked, awaiting approval, timed out or in conflict with the larger delivery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: partial/failed child work, blocked writes, pending approvals, timeout, model/role mismatch, conflicting returned conclusions, and changing current commitments across a multi-child batch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the child portfolio and per-child task/role/model/write commitments, then use the returned whole-batch outcome to resume a lane by taskid, re-dispatch with changed leases, perform an approval/write itself, resolve conflicts or proceed to final delivery. - Decision owner: the main-session model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: batch outcome aggregation, structured termination/approval/blocked-write status, persisted lane transcripts and resumable lane ids, concurrency gate, timeout/recovery machinery, parent system prompt and main-session tool loop. - Closure path: main model creates current child commitments → child lanes execute under S2 leases → orchestrator returns one ordered batch summary covering every child&#39;s result/status → the main model is explicitly instructed not to treat blocked/incomplete outcomes as complete and can revise current commitments through another tool round → subsequent child/main operation follows the revised decision. - Boundary reachability: all elements are wired into the ordinary first-party invokesubagent path and main chat loop; no development-only supervisor is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic concurrency/timeout machinery enforces limits, but the main model chooses the actual task portfolio, roles/models/write scopes and exception response. Removing it leaves no actor making the same task-specific whole-job interventions. - Evidence: subagentguidance.md, SubagentOrchestrator.kt, SubagentLaneRunner.kt, HarnessLoop.kt. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: simple single-agent runs need not exercise S3; the state is established by the standard multi-subagent mode. - Whole-system current view: the parent receives the ordered outcome set for the entire dispatched batch, including each task&#39;s success, termination, tool-call count, pending approvals, blocked writes, read-only/write-intent condition and resumable lane identity, alongside its own current session context. - Current-control decision scope: create/withhold child commitments, choose role/model and write authority, resume a specific lane, redispatch failed/blocked work, personally complete approval/write obligations, reconcile returned conflicts and decide whether the current job is ready to close.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge a child S1&#39;s self-reported completion using a host-side evidence path that sees independently persisted operational receipts rather than trusting the child summary alone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a child model can claim completion even though required verification commands never succeeded, claimed files were not actually written, or a criterion is manually unverifiable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine whether each declared acceptance criterion is backed by host-observed successful command/file-write receipts and downgrade unsupported complete to partial before the claim reaches parent control. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party host adjudication; no autonomous independent auditor owns the verdict at this ref, so the audit path is classified C rather than A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persisted lane transcript, extraction of successful ToolCall/ToolResult command and write receipts, criterion parser, downgrade-only adjudicator and parent-facing adjudication rendering. - Closure path: child emits optional completion claim → host independently reads persisted execution receipts → criteria are reconciled against those receipts → unsupported completion is downgraded → adjudicated result is included in the parent-facing subagent outcome → main S3 sees the corrected operational claim rather than the child&#39;s unsupported status. - Boundary reachability: claim parsing/adjudication is first-party runtime code used by SubagentOrchestrator on ordinary child results; it does not depend on tests or an external evaluator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the complementary evidence channel and corrective return are operationally present, but the decisive audit verdict is fixed host logic rather than an autonomous audit actor. The implementation therefore provides a function-specific constructor/audit path without autonomous S3 ownership. - Evidence: SubagentClaim.kt, SubagentOrchestrator.kt, persisted lane transcript in SubagentLaneRunner.kt/session storage. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: claims without a valid claim block fail open to legacy behavior; C reflects the available first-party audit path, not universal auditing of every child response. - Claim being audited: the child model&#39;s assertion that a delegated subtask is complete and its acceptance criteria have actually been satisfied. - Ordinary reporting path: the child lane&#39;s own textual conclusion/status returned through normal subagent summary. - Complementary access path: host-persisted successful tool execution receipts for commands and structured file writes, which are not controlled by the child claim text. - Independence boundary: the child produces the claim, while host runtime derives receipts from persisted execution events and applies downgrade-only rules; the host never upgrades an unsupported child claim. - Who acts on findings: the host modifies the status before returning it, and the main S3 receives the adjudicated result for follow-up/re-dispatch or final-delivery control.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: turn evidence from completed user-facing work into a prospective option to evolve TaiXu&#39;s reusable skill capability for future tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated/reusable user workflows not covered by current skills, or operational experience revealing defects in an existing reusable skill&#39;s instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept or reject the proposed capability adaptation and, when accepted, create/update the persistent custom skill used by future operation. - Decision owner: the human operator through the first-party skill-suggestion card/apply action; the model-backed advisor develops the adaptation option but does not autonomously install it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: post-run gating after successful tool-heavy work, conversation digest, current skill inventory, separate advisor model call, normalized create/update proposal, SkillSuggestion message/card, SkillSuggestionActions, and AgentSkillRepository.addCustom. - Closure path: external user work exposes a reusable workflow or skill defect → after the successful run the advisor explicitly reasons about future reuse/current skill coverage → it emits a create/update adaptation option → human accepts through the standard Chat UI → the first-party repository persists the new/updated enabled custom skill → future agent operation can use the changed capability. - Boundary reachability: HarnessLoop is wired with SkillEvolutionAdvisor in the packaged runtime and the standard Chat UI exposes apply/dismiss actions whose apply path persists the resulting skill; no repository-development workflow is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the advisor autonomously formulates an adaptation proposal, but the decisive right to alter persistent capability belongs to the human. Because that parent decision is first-party and returns into active skill capability, the published state is P, not A. - Evidence: SkillEvolutionAdvisor.kt, SkillSuggestionActions.kt, ChatViewModel.kt, current skill repository wiring. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: generic memory/learning and current-task web research are not the S4 witness. The credited witness is specifically the post-run future-reuse/skill-gap analysis plus human-governed persistent capability update. - External distinction: completed user-facing work reveals an externally sourced workflow demand or skill defect that the current reusable skill inventory does not adequately cover. - Future / prospective distinction: the advisor explicitly asks whether the just-observed workflow should be reusable in future tasks or whether an existing skill should be evolved for future operation. - Adaptation option generated: a normalized create or update skill proposal containing persistent future instructions, description and optional trigger. - Path back into current capability / S3: the human applies the proposal through the standard Chat UI; AgentSkillRepository.addCustom persists the new/updated enabled skill so subsequent agent operation can select/use the changed capability.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the assessed deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate surfaces inspected included tool approvals, safety/permission modes, system prompts, model/provider settings, workflow approvals and human UI controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no path was found for an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue to reach a legitimate authority and return as a durable highest-level policy/identity decision governing later S1–S4 operation. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordinary human approvals, permission modes, static system prompts/configuration, session/model settings and workflow gates. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary approval of tools, rewinds, skill adaptation or workflows regulates action/capability but does not establish ultimate identity/policy closure. - Evidence: HarnessLoop.kt, approval/runtime policy surfaces, standard prompt/settings paths. - Basis: scoped absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cdf0b13df481624de8574fa9220e7e95bfc705d5/assessments/taixu.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cdf0b13df481624de8574fa9220e7e95bfc705d5\">cdf0b13 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T16:49:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gczhuang-cmd/NovaCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NovaCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gczhuang-cmd/NovaCode/tree/34a2d555a530120fd6241f7065d49390e52f3573\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 34a2d555a530120fd6241f7065d49390e52f3573\">34a2d55 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous repository investigation and modification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code defects, command results and changing local file contents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next tool action or final response after observing prior results. - Decision owner: the main or worker model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, tool registry, permission engine, shell and conversation storage. - Closure path: model tool calls execute and results are appended to the conversation for the next request. - Boundary reachability: installed CLI registers the tools and constructs the first-party Agent; Team launches use that same implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model discretion leaves execution/permission machinery without the coding decisions. - Evidence: loop and result feedback, worker construction. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source-backed availability, not successful provider-backed execution or coding performance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: keep independent coding cells from overwriting one another&#39;s working files. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent agents editing a shared checkout and reading stale parent paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select isolated worker execution and a later integration/conflict-resolution policy. - Decision owner: shipped worktree construction policy establishes separate execution contexts; a developer/operator must compose the discretionary coordination/integration actor beyond the enforced isolation path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Git worktrees, per-agent cwd injection, branch naming, change-preserving cleanup and mailbox/file locks. - Closure path: creation gives the worker a separate checkout and explicit local reread instruction; its tools run in that cwd and retained changes/branch return to the parent. - Boundary reachability: AgentTool&#39;s worktree branch and Team spawn explicitly call the supplied Worktree Manager; this is not a generic mailbox inference. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: worker isolation follows a preselected implementation/definition; no independent agent is shown owning the full interference-specific coordination policy merely because a Lead delegates. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently runnable Team members or parent and background operational agents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: shared-file write collision and stale reads between their repository operations. - Attenuating coordination relation: separate per-worker Git worktrees with cwd-context injection and preserved-result reporting. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: each worker is told to reread locally and operates in the isolated directory; the parent receives the retained worktree identity. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the implementation changes the actual editing substrate to prevent cross-worker overwrite; task dependencies and messaging alone are not credited. - Evidence: worktree execution and notices, Team worktree creation, stated concurrency purpose and integration boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: worktrees do not resolve semantic merge conflicts; specification claims of autonomous convergence are not promoted into an independently established S2=A loop.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current team commitments and permit or withhold worker implementation on behalf of the session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfinished/blocked team work, member readiness and proposed plans that need revision before changing code. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the Lead reads team state, changes assignment/status/dependencies and approves or rejects a member plan with feedback. - Decision owner: the model-driven Team Lead. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent task store, mailbox delivery, wakeup, lead-only approval check and worker permission/tool filtering. - Closure path: task/member notifications reach a Lead turn; its structured approval returns through the mailbox and changes worker permission mode/tools, while rejection requests a revised plan. - Boundary reachability: CLI registers TeamTask/TeamSendMessage and Team Agent spawn; TUI starts lead-mail consumption and wakeup. Optional Coordinator is supplied with these same tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Python enforces who may approve and applies the result; it does not choose whether a plan should be approved or what current commitment should change. - Whole-system current view: current Team tasks/status/assignees/dependencies, membership and member messages/idle notifications. - Current-control decision scope: current team work allocation and implementation permission, not identity or prospective strategic policy. - Evidence: CLI registration, task revision, Lead authority and delivery, returned plan effect, Lead feedback. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: cooperative agent judgment with bounded telemetry, not proof of global optimization; generic user approval and /team kill do not independently establish a parent S3 mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: optional source-grounded challenge to the coding session&#39;s changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: errors/security defects omitted from the producer&#39;s account. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and inspect raw changed files and judge defects in an independent-context review. - Decision owner: the forked review model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in skill catalog, fork executor, fresh Conversation, tool whitelist and report insertion. - Closure path: the review agent&#39;s final findings are inserted into the main conversation, available to subsequent coding turns and operator control. - Boundary reachability: the packaged built-in review skill is registered through the CLI skills path and executed by the shipped fork executor; no custom external reviewer must be built. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic fork/return machinery transports the independently reasoned findings rather than choosing defects or severity. - Claim being audited: the correctness and safety of repository changes made by the coding session. - Ordinary reporting path: the producer&#39;s main conversation/final narrative and tool reports. - Complementary access path: fresh reviewer uses git diff/status and direct file/reference reads. - Independence boundary: forkcontext none, separate conversation, explicit no-modification instruction; same provider/host is not a blind external audit. - Who acts on findings: parent coding session/operator; report enters subsequent control without automatic acceptance or guaranteed remediation. - Evidence: packaged review definition, fresh fork and return. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: optional bounded code audit; bash whitelist is not a security sandbox, and no independent human audit is claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supplied external/prospective adaptation loop into present harness capability established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: memory/context and current task information, rather than future environmental change at this recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced for prospective capability adaptation. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory storage, compaction, skills/MCP loading and task planning. - Closure path: local context reuse is present; external distinction to adaptation option to current capability closure is absent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research/plan roles and persistence are not sufficient evidence of that organizational function. - Evidence: runtime context state, Coordinator current-task phases, architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: an operator can extend skills or choose new providers; that is not a shipped S4 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary action permissions and task plans. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity-level right routed and returned in the supplied mode. - Decision owner: operator/developer configures rules; Lead approves operational plans. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: blacklist, sandbox, permission modes, hooks and Coordinator feature gates. - Closure path: action/plan permissions affect tools, but are not identity/ultimate-policy deliberation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither static safety rules nor Lead operational approval establishes S5. - Evidence: mode configuration, plan return, permission architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: users legitimately own their tasks; no general claim about their wider organization follows.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6102e73ec2bf24b8672a7bedd87657225de7fe37/assessments/no/novacode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6102e73ec2bf24b8672a7bedd87657225de7fe37\">6102e73 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T10:15:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/caelis-labs/caelis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Caelis</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/caelis-labs/caelis/tree/5aebbc6a750b8b2d472a0987fbf2e0942062c6d9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5aebbc6a750b8b2d472a0987fbf2e0942062c6d9\">5aebbc6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-directed engineering work through repeated model-selected tool actions against the workspace and other admitted resources, incorporating tool/environment observations until the task concludes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user goals, repository/workspace state, command and file outcomes, external tool/provider responses, approval/sandbox conditions, task failures and follow-up input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive model/tool action and its task-specific arguments, then revise later action from returned evidence. - Decision owner: the active Caelis Agent model in the main Runtime; each started collaborator independently owns the same local operational discretion within its assigned task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent SDK Runtime, tool definitions/execution wrappers, Session/event persistence, sandbox/policy and approval gates, task lifecycle, compaction and Control-owned runtime composition. - Closure path: user/session context → model chooses action/tool → Runtime validates and executes or requests approval → result/event is persisted and returned into model context → the Agent chooses its next action or final response. - Boundary reachability: this is the normal shipped Caelis Runtime assembled by the product; no eval or developer-only runner is required to exercise the model/tool loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy/sandbox/approval machinery can constrain an attempted action, but removing the model removes the task-specific choice of what operational action to take next and how to respond to its result. - Evidence: agent-sdk/runtime/runtime.go; app/gatewayapp/internal/promptassembly/prompt.go; docs/agent-sdk-boundary.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrently operating participant S1 units by limiting parallel delegation to independent work, assigning task-specific scope/edit authority and allowing explicit steering/integration when participant work interacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple participant Agents can otherwise perform dependent or overlapping work in the same delivery workspace, duplicate effort, act on stale assumptions or create conflicting effects/results that cannot be safely integrated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which work is sufficiently independent to run as a collaborator, define its self-contained goal/scope/constraints/edit permission, and send task-specific follow-up/steering when participant findings require coordination. - Decision owner: the main controller Agent model. The shipped prompt makes independence and integration its responsibility; the StartThread schema exposes the scope/edit-permission decision to that model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Control-authenticated participant topology, Session execution fencing, revision/fence requirements for overlapping writes, StartThread, SendMessage, shared mailbox/roster, public-result observation and durable Session/task identities. - Closure path: controller model identifies independent work and specifies scope/edit permission → participant S1 executes within that assignment → shared messages or thread outcomes expose relevant interaction/results → controller can steer with SendMessage, wait/read the participant and change subsequent participant/main work → controller performs integration/validation before user delivery. - Boundary reachability: the independence rule is injected into the shipped main-Agent system prompt and StartThread/mail/thread tools are part of the first-party collaboration runtime; no downstream coordinator is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic fencing/revision rules enforce effect safety, but they do not decide task-specific independence, participant scope or how returned findings should alter concurrent work. Those coordination judgments belong to the controller model. - Evidence: app/gatewayapp/internal/promptassembly/prompt.go; agent-sdk/tool/builtin/spawn/tool.go; control/collaboration/tools.go; docs/agent-sdk-boundary.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Caelis does not expose TaiXu-style per-child write leases or a global conflict scheduler. S2 credit rests on the shipped model-owned independent-work/scope/steering relation plus concrete shared-workspace effect fencing, not on generic messaging alone. - Distinct S1 units: the main controller Agent plus two or more independently running participant Agent conversations created with StartThread, each executing its own assigned task and returning a public result. - Inter-S1 disturbance: dependent or overlapping participant work can duplicate effort, use conflicting assumptions or attempt overlapping workspace effects; the runtime explicitly treats overlapping writes as requiring a matching revision/fence. - Attenuating coordination relation: the controller is instructed to spawn only independent work, defines each participant&#39;s scope/edit permission, can exchange targeted messages/steering and retains integration/validation responsibility; runtime revision/fence rules enforce concurrent effect consistency. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: participant mail/status/results return through SendMessage/ReadThread/WaitThread; the controller uses them to steer or revise later participant/main action and to decide what can be integrated into final delivery. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited path is not the mailbox by itself; it is the explicit independent-work and scope/edit-permission relation tied to a structurally evidenced shared-workspace concurrency disturbance and returned steering/integration feedback.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain current control over the Session&#39;s active participant organization by creating commitments, observing current participant state/results, steering work and deciding how participant outcomes are integrated into the live user task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing participant roster/state, running versus blocked/attention-needing/completed work, partial or conflicting findings, participant capabilities, newly discovered facts and the need to reallocate current task commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create or withhold participant commitments, select participant/task/scope, observe live thread state/results, send follow-up/steering, wait for selected work and decide whether to integrate, redirect or continue the current job. - Decision owner: the main controller Agent model for task-specific current-control decisions; Control authenticates the current controller epoch and enforces exclusive controller capabilities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ListThreads, controller-only ReadThread/WaitThread, StartThread, SendMessage, current-controller grants, participant/task state, Session persistence, Control-owned placement/lifecycle and controller-epoch enforcement. - Closure path: controller creates a set of current participant commitments → participants execute and update status/results/messages → controller observes roster plus selected current results/attention states → controller steers, waits, launches changed work or integrates outcomes → later S1 execution follows the revised current-control decision. - Boundary reachability: controller-only capabilities are wired into the standard first-party collaboration surface and injected into the parent Agent; they are not test-only lifecycle APIs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Control owns topology security, placement and controller selection, but does not choose the task-specific portfolio, delegation content, steering or integration response. Removing the controller model leaves enforcement but not materially the same discretionary current-control decisions. - Evidence: control/collaboration/controller.go; control/collaboration/tools.go; docs/participants.md; docs/agent-sdk-boundary.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: model-facing collaboration does not expose an arbitrary hard kill/reassignment API; S3 is established through commitment creation, current whole-roster/thread observation, steering and integration authority rather than deterministic cancellation machinery. - Whole-system current view: ListThreads exposes the Session participant roster; controller-only ReadThread returns a participant&#39;s latest public result and status, while WaitThread waits across selected threads for completion or attention. Together these let the controller inspect the current operational portfolio rather than only a final batch result. - Current-control decision scope: choose active participant commitments and their task/scope, send steering or follow-up input where supported, wait/read selected work, start changed/additional participant work and decide how current outputs affect the main task&#39;s integration and completion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently review current workspace changes through a separate Reviewer Agent/context rather than relying only on the main Agent&#39;s own completion/reporting path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the main operational Agent may miss correctness defects, regressions, maintainability/architecture issues or inadequate tests in the current staged/unstaged/untracked workspace delta. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the current workspace change from the fixed Reviewer scene and produce a separate findings judgment that can challenge the main Agent&#39;s ordinary delivery assessment. - Decision owner: the configured Reviewer model or ACP Agent owns the review judgment once invoked; however, standard audit initiation is the user /review path, so an autonomous system-owned invocation/closure is not established at this ref. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed reviewer scene/instructions, separately materialized Reviewer placement, persistent background Reviewer Task, separate child context and follow-up routing. - Closure path: user invokes /review → Control starts a persistent Reviewer with the fixed workspace-review prompt → Reviewer independently inspects and reports findings → the result is available in the same Session for human/controller follow-up and can change subsequent work. The first-party audit path exists, but autonomous invocation is left open. - Boundary reachability: /review is a shipped Caelis control surface and Reviewer materialization is first-party runtime code supporting either a configured model or ACP Agent; no repository CI reviewer is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment itself is made by a distinct Reviewer Agent rather than deterministic parsing, but the ordinary path does not give the operating organization an independent autonomous right to trigger that audit. This is therefore C, not A. - Evidence: internal/controlprompt/review.go; internal/controlprompt/appserveradapter/participant.go; app/gatewayapp/reviewscene.go; docs/participants.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Guardian is not credited here; the architecture explicitly limits Guardian to approval/risk review and states that it does not independently audit task completion. - Claim being audited: that the current workspace delta is correct, regression-safe, maintainable, architecturally sound and sufficiently tested for delivery. - Ordinary reporting path: the main Agent executes the user task, performs its own verification/integration and returns the user-facing result through its normal model/tool Session. - Complementary access path: a separately materialized Reviewer Agent receives a fixed prompt to inspect staged, unstaged and untracked workspace changes and lead with concrete findings. - Independence boundary: separate Reviewer model/ACP placement, background Task and retained child context; it is not the main Agent merely re-reading its own final answer. Invocation remains user-owned at the standard /review surface. - Who acts on findings: the human/controller can continue the Reviewer and change subsequent main/participant work based on findings; the standard path does not itself grant Reviewer authority to commit or close the parent task.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop was established at the reviewed standard-distribution boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established for an S4 loop. Caelis can search/use external tools and can organize memory, but those mechanisms serve current work or internal recall rather than a future-oriented environmental adaptation function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for changing present organizational capability from an external/prospective adaptation judgment. - Decision owner: none established for S4 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MCP/tool discovery, configurable models/agents, skills, memory Remember/Recall, optional Memory Steward and Memory Verifier, runtime configuration snapshots and ordinary human configuration. - Closure path: no qualifying external/prospective signal → adaptation option → present capability change closure was found in the shipped runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool selection, retrieval, semantic memory organization and provider configuration can improve current execution context, but no evidenced first-party actor owns a distinct outside-and-then adaptation judgment whose result changes the organization&#39;s future capability. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; docs/agent-sdk-boundary.md; app/gatewayapp/internal/promptassembly/prompt.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the reviewed Session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: static identity, user objectives, sandbox/approval constraints, controller selection and configuration are present, but no runtime issue path decides what the organization ultimately is or which ultimate policy should govern it. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5 identity/ultimate-policy judgment. - Decision owner: none established for S5 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in identity/workflow prompts, user/workspace instruction precedence, sandbox/policy registry, Guardian/manual approvals, Control-owned controller selection/handoff and operator configuration. - Closure path: ordinary user/configuration/approval inputs can constrain subsequent actions, but no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy issue is routed to a legitimate S5 authority and returned as authoritative organizational policy closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main model interprets tasks within a predefined identity/policy envelope; it does not own authority to redefine that envelope. Control enforces controller/policy state but no first-party runtime process establishes a separate ultimate-policy judgment loop. - Evidence: app/gatewayapp/internal/promptassembly/prompt.go; docs/agent-sdk-boundary.md; docs/architecture.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5904d1035156475161d7e84a724fb41efdf138f9/assessments/caelis.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5904d1035156475161d7e84a724fb41efdf138f9\">5904d10 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T01:25:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DeepSeek Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/tree/ddefc45fbc7f8e46dd73185e68295696d1297887\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ddefc45fbc7f8e46dd73185e68295696d1297887\">ddefc45 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: autonomously pursue repository/general tasks through repeated model decisions, tool calls, Session state and subsequent turns. - Decisive right / owner: the model-driven Agent chooses operational actions from the supplied tool surface. - Closure: accepted tool/results and Session events enter later model requests and alter subsequent action. - Supporting mechanisms: Agent loop, Session persistence, tool registry, provider adapters, cancellation and optional subagents. -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A. - Positive mode: published opt-in Agent Teams. - Distinct S1 units: Team Lead plus named teammate Agents working in one shared checkout. - Concrete disturbance: concurrent members can contend over shared files and stale task/assignment state. The first-party design explicitly notes that Bash, generators and external writers can bypass file stale-version fences, so coordination rather than false locking is required. - Coordination relation: - shared tasks have owners, dependency DAGs, readiness and advisory write scopes; - blocked tasks cannot be claimed until dependencies complete; - stale task revisions fail instead of silently overwriting newer assignment state; - overlapping in-progress write scopes are surfaced as warnings; - peer messages steer running members or resume idle/inactive teammates; - the fixed Team policy directs members to partition writes, order dependent work and rebase after stale-file feedback. - Feedback closure: board/list/get results, overlap warnings, rejected stale mutations and peer messages return into Team-member model contexts; members subsequently claim/release/complete work, message peers, or alter task structure. - Decisive right / owner: Agents choose task decomposition, dependencies, write scopes, claims and peer coordination; deterministic CAS/DAG/mailbox machinery enforces those decisions. The organizational coordination right is therefore agent-owned, not merely runtime-owned. - Caveat: write scopes are advisory rather than filesystem locks, so S2 attenuates shared-checkout interference without eliminating every external-write collision. -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P). - Autonomous Team mode: - the Lead can inspect roster/task state; - only the Lead creates and interrupts teammates; - the Lead may assign/reassign tasks across members; - the Team policy requires the Lead to wait for required work, inspect the final diff and run tests before the final answer. - Whole-system current disturbance: active/inactive/stuck workers, assignment gaps, dependency readiness, overlapping intended writes and incomplete Team commitments. - Agent-owned closure: the Lead observes current Team state and can spawn, message, assign/reassign, interrupt, wait and integrate; those interventions change subsequent teammate work and Team commitments. This establishes A. - Parent mode: the published Agent Teams Web profile lets a user inspect the authoritative roster/task board and create, edit, assign/unassign, complete, reopen and delete Team tasks. Mutations use revision-checked Remote methods and the UI reloads the complete Team view after success or conflict. Agents subsequently read the same authoritative task board, so parent decisions return into current operation. This separately establishes first-party parent-governed S3 and yields A(P). - Supporting/enforcement mechanisms: Team service, task CAS/DAG validation, Remote API, browser Client and Session persistence. - Caveat: the Web parent panel cannot itself spawn or interrupt teammates; its parent S3 authority is task/commitment regulation rather than the full Lead lifecycle surface. -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: —. - A teammate can be asked to review a diff, but generic role delegation does not by itself supply a distinct independent audit function. - Experimental Auto Review performs a separate model call before each pending tool body and gates execution according to risk/authorization policy. This is pre-action permission control, not complementary independent access to operational reality after or outside the normal control channel. - Ralph explicitly states that completion/blocker outcomes are worker reports and are not independently verified; its documentation defers an independent evaluator. - No first-party mode was established that supplies a sufficiently independent audit actor with its own operational evidence and audit-to-correction closure. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Web search, goals, workflows, Ralph rounds, Session history and plugin composition can change a current task trajectory. None establishes a distinct first-party function that monitors the external future environment, develops organizational adaptation options and closes them into changed future capability. - Plugin installation/update is operator/deployment configuration rather than autonomous S4. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - Permission presets, Auto Review risk rules, system/persona prompts, plugin/profile selection and explicit-human Team activation are constraints and deployment choices. They do not establish a runtime identity/ultimate-policy function that resolves organizational policy tensions at the declared recursion. - Generic human approval or profile choice is not promoted to parent S5. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b293e7031f149c5f41baedede15b444f983a5f4d/assessments/deepseek-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b293e7031f149c5f41baedede15b444f983a5f4d\">b293e70 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/i3T4AN/KADATH\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">KADATH</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/i3T4AN/KADATH/tree/db7a6438d98c18d590b78b2146dc3bcd2c4ea0ef\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision db7a6438d98c18d590b78b2146dc3bcd2c4ea0ef\">db7a643 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: heritable organism agents perform the user goal by reasoning, using tools, managing durable progress, producing artifacts/results, and optionally delegating bounded subproblems to temporary workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended task/environment variety encountered while trying to satisfy the approved measurement criterion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next reasoning/tool/workspace action and determine the candidate operational result within the organism&#39;s allowed capabilities. - Decision owner: the organism&#39;s model-driven agent loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only genome mount, scoped broker token, workspace/artifact storage, browser/search tools, worker limits, epoch deadline, resource/container isolation, and the kernel&#39;s lifecycle enforcement. - Closure path: observations and tool/worker results return to the organism; the organism updates durable progress/output and continues acting until the epoch finishes. - Boundary reachability: organism execution is the standard population runtime created for every approved KADATH run; it does not rely on a development-only or external harness owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the kernel constrains the action space but does not choose the organism&#39;s task-solving actions; those choices are model-driven inside the shipped organism loop. - Evidence: inside an epoch and worker behavior, research, browsing, and temporary workers. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: temporary workers are subordinate to a parent organism and are not separately credited as S1 units at the declared recursion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination function is established at the declared population recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-organism containers, scoped credentials, separate browser contexts, population ranking, and sequencing isolate or compare organisms but do not by themselves regulate a demonstrated interference/oscillation among cooperating S1 units. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: population plurality, competition, shared benchmark use, and isolation are insufficient to establish S2 without a specific inter-S1 disturbance and attenuation loop. - Evidence: population execution and isolation, isolation and credentials. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a deployment could build cooperative coordination among organisms, but the reviewed standard distribution does not establish the required S2-specific witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: the kernel maintains a whole-run current-control path over population status and applies ranking, survival/replacement, execution limits, and activation decisions that regulate the current evolutionary organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: population failure, uneven verified performance, invalid/duplicate adaptation, runaway execution, and loss of a viable active population. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine which verified genomes remain active, which are culled/replaced, and which valid post-grade adaptations become the next active population under the fixed current-control policy. - Decision owner: the standard runtime exposes this S3-specific control path through developer-authored deterministic kernel policy; no autonomous agent owns the decisive population-control policy in the reviewed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: frozen grading results, deterministic score/rank computation, population-size rules, content-addressed genomes, worktree validation, resource limits, and activation/cleanup machinery. - Closure path: verified outcomes and ranks enter the kernel selection path; the selected survivors/adaptations/children become the active population for the next epoch. - Boundary reachability: the selection/activation path is built into the standard kernel and runs after every non-final graded epoch; it is not a CI or example-only construction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tweaker/Birther and middle agents can propose adaptations, but the whole-population current-control right remains in fixed kernel selection/activation rules. KADATH therefore supplies a concrete S3 construction path without an autonomous S3 owner. - Whole-system current view: the kernel has run/epoch identities, frozen grading outcomes, ranks, lineage, population membership, crashes, and active genome state for the whole population. - Current-control decision scope: population survival/replacement/activation and enforcement of run-wide execution constraints; deterministic enforcement is recorded separately from model-driven adaptation proposals. - Evidence: evidence freezing and grading, selection and evolution, kernel implementation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: C does not credit hard enforcement as autonomous management. It records the first-party function-specific current-control path while preserving that the decisive policy is developer-authored rather than agent-owned.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: a separate model Grader inspects a frozen evidence boundary rather than ordinary organism self-report, makes rubric/anti-fraud/failure judgments, and returns those findings into population control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: fabricated, unsupported, fraudulent, crashed, or otherwise invalid claims of task success and misleading organism self-scores. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make the semantic audit judgments for rubric facts, automatic failures, anti-fraud checks, tie-break facts, and cited evidence from the frozen attempt. - Decision owner: the model-driven Grader. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: kernel-owned evidence freezing, fragment coverage/checksums, reference validation, integrity seals, and deterministic locked formula application. - Closure path: Grader findings are validated by the kernel, converted through locked formulas into verified outcome/score/rank, and directly affect selection, culling, and reproduction for the next population. - Boundary reachability: the Grader/frozen-evidence path is part of every standard grading phase, separate from the evolvable organism genome and reachable without borrowing a development-only reviewer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the kernel does not itself make the semantic evidence judgments; it freezes evidence, validates references, and applies formulas. Removing the Grader leaves those discretionary audit judgments absent. - Claim being audited: the organism&#39;s claimed task outcome and evidence satisfy the approved benchmark without triggering failure/anti-fraud rules. - Ordinary reporting path: organism result envelope, workspace/artifacts, high-level activity, tool/model traces, worker outputs, and provisional result material produced during execution. - Complementary access path: kernel-created immutable frozen attempt including artifacts, traces, browser/worker evidence, crash/identity metadata, exact objective/genome hashes, and optional independent measurement facts. - Independence boundary: the Grader operates after all parents/workers are stopped, against frozen evidence; organisms cannot rewrite the benchmark, score, scheduler, lineage, or frozen post-run evidence and self-scores are explicitly ignored. - Who acts on findings: the kernel validates the audit references, computes locked scores/ranks, and uses them in population selection and reproduction. - Evidence: evidence freezing and grading, selection and evolution. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the deterministic kernel owns formula application and enforcement, not the model Grader&#39;s audit judgment; this split is why the audit is not inferred merely from the component name Grader.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: the evolutionary adaptation loop turns verified environment/benchmark performance and evidence from the current population into changed agent frameworks for future epochs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mismatch between present organism capability and the externally measured performance demanded by the locked task environment/benchmark. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose adaptation proposals for future capability: middle organisms may mutate or remain unchanged; Tweaker/Birther paths derive reproduction guidance and mutated children from verified population evidence. - Decision owner: model-driven organism/specialist adaptation actors within the shipped evolutionary loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: kernel grading/ranking, elite dossiers and snapshots, population/own/inherited memory, mutation validation, duplicate rejection, Git lineage, and next-epoch activation. - Closure path: verified performance/evidence informs model-driven mutation/reproduction choices; valid changed genomes are committed and activated; subsequent epochs execute those changed capabilities against the environment. - Boundary reachability: middle-agent reflection and Tweaker/Birther reproduction are standard post-grade phases of the shipped run rather than separate development tooling. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic selection and mutation safeguards constrain candidates, but the substantive adaptation proposals are produced by model-driven actors from verified evidence. - External distinction: observed, frozen, independently graded performance and evidence under the approved benchmark/tool/environment configuration. - Future / prospective distinction: which framework characteristics, memories, and mutations are likely to improve performance in subsequent epochs. - Adaptation option generated: preserve, mutate prompt/source/dependencies/files, or reproduce a mutated descendant from an elite genome. - Path back into current capability / S3: the kernel validates and commits accepted mutations, restores the configured population, and activates the changed genome set for the next epoch. - Evidence: middle-agent reflection, Tweaker and Birther, memory and heredity. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this credit is not based on the label evolution; it depends on a closed future-capability loop from measured environment performance to changed genomes that actually run later.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish and lock the run-level definition of success, permitted evidence/tools, anti-fraud constraints, and measurement policy that governs every organism and evolutionary selection decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguity or drift in what the evolutionary organization is optimizing and what evidence/behavior counts as legitimate success. - Decisive decision or feedback right: approve or reject the Architect&#39;s proposed benchmark before activation; once approved, the benchmark/objective/tool/runtime hashes are locked for the run. - Decision owner: the human operator as parent authority for the run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Architect proposal generation, structured benchmark contract, preflight, immutable hash locks, and runtime refusal to continue after locked-input tampering. - Closure path: operator approval activates and locks the policy; the locked benchmark subsequently governs grading, ranking, selection, and all future epochs. Rejection leaves the proposed run inactive. - Boundary reachability: benchmark proposal and explicit approval are part of the standard interactive launch path, not repository governance or an adjacent development process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Architect proposes policy content but does not hold ultimate activation authority; there is no evidenced standard mode in which an agent can unilaterally redefine and authorize the run&#39;s ultimate fitness policy. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what the whole evolutionary run exists to optimize, what measurements define success, and which anti-fraud/tool/evidence constraints bind the population. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent mode only — the human operator approves the benchmark that becomes authoritative for the run. - Return-to-operation path: approved benchmark and hashes become immutable runtime inputs to grading, ranking, and evolutionary selection across all epochs. - Evidence: Architect and benchmark locking, run setup and approval. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary organism result approval is not being called S5. The credited parent decision is the run-wide, persistent fitness/legitimacy policy that controls the identity of the evolutionary process.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/kadath.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T19:38:34+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nickprotop/cxagent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CXAgent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nickprotop/cxagent/tree/e2b2551fc8897d5be5833441e9ec6e3c4e63cdf1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e2b2551fc8897d5be5833441e9ec6e3c4e63cdf1\">e2b2551 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: interpret a coding request, read/modify files, run tools/tests, handle failures and adapt the next step. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific requirements, repository contents, tests and shell/tool results, permissions, model messages, tool errors and background task outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select next tool action or delegate child operation and revise based on observed result. - Decision owner: root model in a normal coding session; bounded child models in fan-out mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: .NET host, model provider transport, session persistence, file/shell job executor, permission layer and child spawner. - Closure path: operator request → root model → first-party permission-gated tool/delegate action → file/test/child feedback → model correction or output. - Boundary reachability: included in the supported executable cxagent terminal session, and in CxAgent.Core as composed by that session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model chooses task-specific actions and responses; deterministic host only executes and constrains them. - Evidence: cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/Agent.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/AgentHost.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Jobs/Builtin/FileJobExecutor.cs, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: third-party model/provider is the decision engine reached through first-party harness, not a separate imported organization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent S1 code-writing interference on the same workspace file, making incompatible stale edits visible instead of silently losing one worker&#39;s change. - Distinct S1 units: independent concurrently executing model-driven child agents instantiated by a root session and sharing the shipped file executor/workspace. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous children can read one file, form edits on the same original bytes, then overwrite one another; the first-party commentary records this and a real concurrent-replace regression test. - Attenuating coordination relation: a static per-canonical-path lock spans the entire read-modify-write and stale-content validation; WriteIfUnchangedAsync refuses obsolete edits. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a stale edit produces a specific refusal explaining that nothing was written and instructing reread/revise; that tool result enters the child&#39;s model feedback loop, allowing a new edit against current bytes, and parent sees outcomes. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism directly handles conflicting writes by distinct simultaneous coding S1s on one shared artifact, not merely dispatching messages or serializing unrelated jobs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: races in shared-file mutation and loss of independent children&#39;s implemented changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coordinate which writer may hold the file path, reject stale operation and return corrective signal; agent chooses subsequent remediation, not lock policy itself. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic FileMutation chooses lock/admission; autonomous children can respond to returned conflict, but the S2-specific policy choice is not dynamically agent owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static SemaphoreSlim map, file snapshots, StaleContentException, shared FileJobExecutor. - Closure path: competing child edits → per-path exclusion/validation → conflict refusal or sequential safe result → next child-model correction on returned evidence. - Boundary reachability: first-party normal replaceinfile/write operations and fan-out children share this executable file mutation path; not a dev-only test helper. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the structural interference response is supplied and selected by deterministic shipped code; C credits a function-specific constructor path, not autonomous S2 policy ownership. - Evidence: cxagent.Core/Core/Jobs/Builtin/FileMutation.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Jobs/Builtin/FileJobExecutor.cs, cxagent.Tests/TwoSessionsTests.cs, cxagent.Tests/FileJobExecutorTests.cs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: per-file safety is narrower than negotiated scheduling over whole-repository edits. Across distinct processes the in-process lock does not apply; stale-content check remains a separate guard.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: within one multi-child session, inspect currently committed child/background work and intervene on present operational commitments or available resources. - Whole-system current view: model-facing agentlist reports retained child identities, specialist types and busy state; joblist reports all live background commands, ownership, timing, output paths and concurrency use across the session. - Current-control decision scope: root model may use agentsend to redirect an existing child with preserved context, deliver to busy child mailbox, or kill any session/child-created background job rather than simply wait for completion; it can choose follow-on delegation and priority in response to statuses. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runaway/surplus background work, stale or blocked commitments, changing task priorities and subsidiary agents requiring correction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select operational intervention, new instruction or job termination using whole-session current facts, not only run a static queue. - Decision owner: autonomous root model when it invokes the bundled live view/control tools in fan-out mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child store, busy-claim mutex, mailbox, process registry, runtime permission and job ownership checks. - Closure path: child/work registry state → agentlist/joblist → root model&#39;s follow-up/jobkill decision → child mailbox/future turn or terminated background process → updated current operation. - Boundary reachability: all named tools are offered by first-party root-agent runtime in the shipped fan-out/session mode, not hypothetical external dashboard APIs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parent chooses which child to redirect or process to stop, while runtime only keeps the list/ownership boundaries and enforces specific actions. - Evidence: cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/AgentReachTools.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/SubAgentStore.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/AgentMailbox.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Jobs/BackgroundJobTools.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/Agent.cs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: synchronous tool result barrier means the model may not inspect every executing child continuously during the original spawn turn; the credited current-control path is session-local, based on retained children, mailbox and concurrent background jobs. Not global control across separate UI sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: a separately running bundled review child checks actual source/diff evidence and challenges the implementer&#39;s correctness claim before the parent acts on findings. - Claim being audited: another agent or root implementer has produced code that is correct for the requested change. - Ordinary reporting path: author&#39;s own completion report, observed tool/test outputs and normal coding trace. - Complementary access path: distinct first-party child Agent with fresh conversation, builtin review type and direct read/grep/shell tools inspects named changed files or diff and returns specific objections with failing cases. - Independence boundary: the built-in type is supplied by product code, receives its own model context and can inspect artifact bytes rather than relying solely on original developer&#39;s narration. It may use the same base provider/model; independence here is execution/context and evidence access, not guaranteed distinct vendor. - Who acts on findings: root model receives review subagent result in normal tool feedback and may modify code, run checks or redirect work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: silent correctness defects, incomplete implementation and overconfident author claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose an artifact-grounded judgment independently of implementation context, return objections as new information for corrective control. - Decision owner: reviewer child model for its audit judgment; parent owns corrective task choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: builtin review profile/type, separate child context, shared read tools, first-party subagent-result envelope and parent continuation. - Closure path: parent requests independent review → first-party reviewer reads operational artifacts → model reports concrete verified issues → parent model receives and can correct subsequent implementation. - Boundary reachability: review is a shipped built-in agent type in BuiltinAgentTypes.cs; ordinary agent subagent factory/spawner can instantiate it in product&#39;s standard fan-out mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: child model makes the differential correctness judgment from direct workspace evidence; deterministic return path does not choose its findings. - Evidence: cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/BuiltinAgentTypes.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/SubAgentSpawner.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/SubAgentFactory.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/Agent.cs, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: role invocation is optional and parent-chosen, not an always-on merge admission or externally guaranteed neutral auditor. Distinct context and raw file access, not naming alone, underpin this classification.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no evidenced first-party closed external-prospective adaptation loop changing the system&#39;s future organizational capability at this chosen session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as organizational S4; task-local web fetch, model selection and extension loading address present work or developer/user-supplied capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no supported agent-owned future-environment interpretation and returned capability adaptation identified. - Decision owner: no S4 owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web/HTTP fetch tools, session context, model catalogs, skills and plugin loading, user configuration, project docs and development roadmap. - Closure path: none qualifying S4; local task results inform immediate coding decisions rather than durable future capability design owned by the agent. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: externally authored extensions and model/provider changes can alter capability but do not prove an agent-led prospective outside-and-then loop in the supported distribution. - Evidence: cxagent.Core/Core/Llm/ProviderCatalog.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/Agent.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Sessions/Session.cs, README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: optional contributor-authored/adaptive plugins not included unless actually wired in a first-party shipped autonomous mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party organizational constitutional arbiter for the system&#39;s enduring purpose/ultimate policy, with authoritative feedback into operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission/trust exceptions and task constraints exist, but identity- or mission-level policy conflicts are not evidenced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none meeting S5 ultimate purpose/identity at selected session recursion. - Decision owner: none established; user and config supply ordinary execution trust and tool permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: folder permissions, trusted commands, model classifier in auto mode, builtin agent type briefings, user-defined config and plugin gates. - Closure path: runtime enforces user-set local rules; no identity/purpose proposal → legitimate authority → constitutional decision → returned policy loop found. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model still leaves configuration and guard rules; removing operator consent does not expose autonomous ultimate-purpose decision right. - Evidence: cxagent.Core/Core/Permissions/PermissionPolicy.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Permissions/PermissionDecider.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/BuiltinAgentTypes.cs, COMMANDS.md, README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external project/human governance is outside this standard session boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2b9b27db39203953df10c81d89bef53e30246e99/assessments/cxagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2b9b27db39203953df10c81d89bef53e30246e99\">2b9b27d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T16:23:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/thinkany-ai/dscode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DSCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/thinkany-ai/dscode/tree/1ce0328cfa856700f6c955f5429ca00b08d99ea5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1ce0328cfa856700f6c955f5429ca00b08d99ea5\">1ce0328 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing software-engineering work through a model-driven coding session that selects tools, observes results and iterates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, repository/file/Git state, command/test/diagnostic output, tool failures, model observations, context pressure, permissions and implementation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what evidence to inspect, what coding/command/patch/delegation action to perform next, how to revise work from results and when to finish the task. - Decision owner: the primary model-backed DSCode coding agent in the supported session runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DSCode Core tool registration; minimal/safe toolsets; patch/checkpoint path; command sandbox; permissions; sessions; plan state; diagnostics; MCP/hooks; CLI/Desktop/RPC hosts. - Closure path: user objective + current session/workspace → model selects tool/action → DSCode executes/gates it → result returns into the coding session → model chooses subsequent action or completes. - Boundary reachability: normal terminal, headless/RPC and Desktop surfaces instantiate the same DSCode Core and coding session without requiring downstream orchestration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed primary actor leaves tools, permission/sandbox and session machinery but removes the open-ended coding judgment that selects and sequences actions. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/dscode-extension.ts; packages/core/src/tools.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider inference and generic coding-session substrate are dependencies; DSCode is credited for the concrete first-party product composition and organizational roles it exposes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive write interference among concurrently executing delegated implementation S1s by assigning them independent Git worktrees. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two or more implementation children editing the same checkout in parallel could overwrite, race with or observe one another&#39;s incomplete changes, making candidate ownership/integration unreliable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which independent delegated tasks receive the implementer role, whose standard semantics include isolated candidate changes rather than shared-workspace mutation. - Decision owner: the primary model-backed DSCode agent calling the standard delegate tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role-bearing delegate schema; explicit model prompt guidelines; four-worker bounded fan-out; per-implementer detached Git worktree creation; per-child cwd; returned candidate diffs; depth-one child boundary. - Closure path: primary agent identifies independent parallel work → assigns implementation tasks to implementer children → DSCode creates a separate worktree for each before execution → children modify isolated repository states → each returns its result/diff → primary agent uses the coordinated outputs for subsequent integration/verification. - Boundary reachability: registerSubagentTools registers delegate in the normal DSCode Core and the README documents it as standard parallel work; no external team orchestrator is required. - Distinct S1 units: two or more concurrently launched implementer child DSCode coding sessions, each capable of bounded model/tool software-engineering work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: parallel implementation children would otherwise share mutable repository state and can overwrite or contaminate one another&#39;s in-progress file changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: DSCode maps the model-selected implementer role to a separate detached Git worktree for each child, removing the shared-write surface while preserving an explicit candidate diff for integration. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: isolation changes each child S1&#39;s actual working environment before it acts; after completion the primary S1 receives every child result/diff and can choose which candidate to integrate or rework. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping rests on a concrete cross-S1 write-collision disturbance and a first-party isolation relation that specifically attenuates that disturbance. Role names, concurrency and delegation alone are not credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: DSCode deterministically enforces implementer isolation, but the primary model owns the task-specific decision to classify/delegate independent work as implementation tasks under that coordination mode. - Evidence: packages/core/src/subagents.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: explorer/reviewer/tester children do not need the same write isolation. S2=A credits the supported agent-owned role/decomposition decision that invokes the interference-specific implementer path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party whole-organization current-control loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the primary agent decomposes work and later receives child outcomes, but reviewed evidence does not expose live descendant-state observation plus substantive mid-run steering/reallocation/commitment control across the child organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S3 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegate task decomposition; concurrency cap; child success/failure/result fan-in; primary integration responsibility; plan/status; permissions and background processes. - Closure path: not applicable; no whole-system current view → current-control intervention → changed active child commitments/resources loop was reconstructed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: initial delegation and final integration remain part of the primary S1&#39;s execution/decomposition. The parent waits for bounded child results rather than acting as a live current-control center over them. - Evidence: packages/core/src/subagents.ts; packages/core/src/dscode-extension.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: “the primary agent owns integration and final validation” is meaningful responsibility but does not by itself establish the Profile&#39;s whole-current S3 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review a producing coding S1&#39;s change using a separate read-only model-backed reviewer and return findings to the primary agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the primary/implementer coding path may produce a change that contains correctness defects, regressions or unsupported assumptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect repository/change evidence in a read-only child context and return a reviewer judgment prioritizing correctness/regressions. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed DSCode child selected with role reviewer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model-callable delegate; reviewer role instructions; child DSCode process; --permission plan; --sandbox read-only; independent child context; result aggregation into the parent tool response. - Closure path: producing S1 has a candidate/change → primary delegates a reviewer task → separate read-only reviewer inspects current repository evidence → reviewer returns findings → DSCode delivers those findings in the delegate result → primary agent can revise/integrate/reject subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: reviewer is a built-in standard role in the normal delegate tool and is documented in the shipped product; no custom downstream reviewer code is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime launches and confines the reviewer, while the semantic review judgment comes from a distinct model-backed child; removing that child removes the complementary audit judgment. - Evidence: packages/core/src/subagents.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: review invocation is model-selected rather than mandatory for every task. - Claim being audited: the correctness/fitness of a code change or candidate implementation produced by the primary/implementation path. - Ordinary reporting path: normal primary/implementer coding output and candidate diff/result. - Complementary access path: a separate reviewer process with read-only permission/sandbox independently inspects repository evidence under explicit correctness/regression review instructions. - Independence boundary: separate child process/model context, role-specific instructions, read-only execution boundary, no nested delegation and no file-editing role. - Who acts on findings: the primary DSCode agent receives the reviewer output in the delegate result and owns subsequent integration/fix/verification decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material outside-and-future intelligence/adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project instructions, skills, model/provider configuration, sessions and diagnostics enrich future operation, but no autonomous prospective environmental-modeling and persistent adaptation-selection loop was reconstructed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS/CLAUDE instructions; Agent Skills; MCP/hooks; model/provider selection; durable sessions; context compaction; diagnostics. - Closure path: not applicable; no external/future distinction → adaptation-option generation → autonomous selection → persistent capability/strategy change → later operation closure was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic ability to edit project instructions/configuration or use skills is not by itself S4. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/dscode-extension.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream workflows can use DSCode to modify durable project configuration; that generic capability is not credited as a closed first-party S4 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy decision loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission modes, sandbox/network boundaries, project trust, hooks and project instructions constrain operation, but they are configured operating policy rather than identity-level governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for a genuine identity/ultimate-policy issue. - Decision owner: user/operator/project configuration for the relevant constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan/ask/auto/full permissions; OS/Docker sandbox; network gating; project trust; approvals; AGENTS/CLAUDE instructions; hooks. - Closure path: not applicable at S5 level; no identity/policy conflict → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned organizational operation loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models operate within selected policy; deterministic enforcement does not become S5 by being strong. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/dscode-extension.ts; packages/core/src/access.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a broader organization can govern DSCode through these controls, but that external parent is not imported without function-specific closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e2aa232f6151393ed27c6eea610c75185389a941/assessments/dscode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e2aa232f6151393ed27c6eea610c75185389a941\">e2aa232 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-22T22:46:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zachary-wilde/reigen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reigen / Conductor</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zachary-wilde/reigen/tree/6236f47a1b2f3081a0f22bcba47705bcd1680720\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6236f47a1b2f3081a0f22bcba47705bcd1680720\">6236f47 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform bounded software-development work for an approved Ravel brief through an external coding-agent process acting in its own project worktree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific repository state, implementation uncertainty, tool/build/test feedback, local errors and the bounded context needed to satisfy the brief. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive reasoning path, code/tool actions and local implementation decisions needed to satisfy the assigned brief within its constraints. - Decision owner: the autonomous external Claude/Codex/Z.AI model actor launched through the first-party child-session path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party harness resolution/launch, worktree and branch lifecycle, role prompt, brief scope, dependency-output publication, optional auto-approval, session/process tracking and operator controls. - Closure path: approved brief becomes ready → Ravel manager selects spawnchild → Conductor creates/binds the worker session and worktree with the role prompt → external model actor chooses task actions and receives repository/tool feedback → child exits/publishes result → outcome returns into Ravel verification/current control. - Boundary reachability: Ravel child dispatch and external CLI launch are normal shipped operating paths in ravel.ts/harness.ts; the autonomous actor is reached directly by the standard product rather than through repository-development tooling. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Conductor constrains and supervises the child, but the substantive task-local choices are made by the external model actor. Worktree/process machinery does not replace that operational discretion. - Evidence: README.md; src/main/ravel.ts; src/main/harness.ts; src/main/ravel-model.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external harness/model internals are not inherited into Conductor. The positive state credits the external autonomous S1 actor only because the first-party standard runtime directly launches and governs it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive file/scope interference among sibling coding workers executing parallel briefs in the same Ravel plan. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel S1 workers can edit overlapping repository paths or enter one another&#39;s intended scope, producing conflicting changes and unstable integration assumptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select concrete per-brief scope-exclusion boundaries that partition parallel work sufficiently to reduce collision risk while retaining useful local autonomy. - Decision owner: the autonomous Ravel manager when it constructs the multi-brief plan and chooses each brief&#39;s doNotTouch boundaries from the mission/repository context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: manager protocol requiring meaningful doNotTouch for fan-out, plan validation rejecting missing boundaries, worktree isolation, dependency gates, role-prompt injection, changed-path observation and Insights overlap/protected-path detection. - Closure path: manager decides to fan work out across distinct briefs → it must choose explicit doNotTouch boundaries for every parallel brief → plan validation preserves those boundaries and the approved plan becomes executable → buildRolePrompt() injects the selected exclusions into each S1 worker → subsequent worker behavior is constrained away from sibling scope; current changed-path observations can surface boundary violations/overlap for further intervention. - Boundary reachability: the coordination rule is embedded in the normal manager prompt and plan validator and is returned into standard child prompts; it is not a documentation-only convention or CI check. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic validation merely refuses an invalid fan-out plan and worktrees merely isolate physical working copies. The manager chooses the semantic separation relation. The human&#39;s exact-plan approval governs whether the whole current plan may execute, but the S2-specific choice of collision-attenuating boundaries is generated by the manager; removing the manager leaves no equivalent adaptive boundary-selection decision. - Evidence: src/main/manager-turn.ts; src/main/ravel-model.ts; src/main/insights/rules.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dependency edges, role names, worktrees and turn ordering are not counted merely from topology. The positive witness is the explicit parallel-edit collision problem plus an agent-selected scope-separation relation that is mandatory in multi-brief plans and returned to each worker. Human ratification of the overall exact plan is S3 parent governance and does not by itself own the narrower S2 boundary-selection judgment. - Distinct S1 units: two or more separately launched coding-agent child sessions associated with different briefs in one approved multi-brief Ravel plan. - Inter-S1 disturbance: parallel workers can touch the same file/scope; the manager protocol states that boundaries are required when there is somebody else to collide with, and the Insights subsystem structurally detects concurrent changed-path overlap/protected-path violations. - Attenuating coordination relation: explicit per-brief doNotTouch scope exclusions selected during autonomous plan construction, with worktree isolation and dependency gating supporting the relation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the selected exclusions are serialized into each worker&#39;s role prompt before execution, so the coordination decision changes what each S1 is instructed not to modify; violations/overlap remain observable for current intervention. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited path is explicitly tied to preventing concrete parallel-agent scope/file collisions, not merely to moving messages or ordering tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate the current Ravel organization as a whole by choosing and revising present work commitments, dispatch/intervention actions and responses to current fleet events. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ready/blocked briefs, child completion/failure, verification results, live worker behavior, capacity, harness availability, detached workers, dependent briefs and other current conditions can require whole-Ravel intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in autonomous mode, decide which approved ready brief to spawn, whether/how to message a live child, how to respond to child/verification/detach events and when the approved fleet is complete; in parent mode, decide whether an exact current plan revision may execute and directly intervene in worker lifecycle/commitments. - Decision owner: base mode — the autonomous Ravel manager invoked on bounded event turns. Parent mode — the human operator/approver using exact-plan approval and Operations Core worker controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bounded reconstructed manager context, current-plan and fleet digests, plan-revision guard, dependency eligibility, process/worktree lifecycle, persistent Core/store, Operations event journal, worker-control availability/confirmation logic and remote transport. - Closure path: current whole-Ravel plan/fleet/event state is assembled → manager or operator makes a current-control decision → first-party tool/control path validates and applies spawn/message/approval/pause/resume/stop/retry/archive/detach/replan consequences → runtime/persistence changes the live organization → new fleet/current state is fed into subsequent event turns or operator views. - Boundary reachability: manager turns and operator controls are both normal supported runtime paths. The manager is invoked directly through the configured external harness in the Ravel operating mode; parent controls are exposed by the desktop/Core/remote operator surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the manager has more than a delegation label: every turn receives the approved mission/plan, dispatch state and live fleet snapshot, and it has current intervention tools. Deterministic dependency, approval and lifecycle checks enforce allowed transitions but do not choose the manager&#39;s intervention. The operator path independently closes the same current-control function in a first-party parent mode. - Evidence: src/main/manager-turn.ts; src/main/ravel.ts; src/main/ravel-model.ts; src/main/operations/core.ts; src/main/operations/worker-controls.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: initial task decomposition alone would not establish S3. The positive mapping relies on the manager&#39;s whole-Ravel current snapshot plus event-driven current intervention/replan path. Exact-plan approval is not treated as S5; at this recursion it is a parent decision over present organizational commitments. - Whole-system current view: the manager prompt carries the current mission constraints, exact plan revision/approval state, every brief&#39;s role/harness/dispatch/dependency state and the live child fleet; the Operations Core additionally exposes persistent normalized worker/timeline state to the operator. - Current-control decision scope: autonomous dispatch and live-child messaging/reaction to current events; parent exact-plan admission plus message/pause/resume/stop/retry/archive/detach controls, with detach explicitly causing a manager replan event around blocked dependents.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently check a finished worker&#39;s ordinary success claim against project-defined verification evidence before the metasystem acts on that exit. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a child can report successful completion even when the repository&#39;s independent verification command fails or cannot run. - Decisive decision or feedback right: define and produce the complementary pass/fail audit judgment over the finished child&#39;s worktree; that judgment must reach current control before the child&#39;s self-report is acted on. - Decision owner: no autonomous S3 owner is packaged by Conductor. The project/operator supplies the repository verification command and therefore the audit criterion/judgment source; Conductor supplies the S3-specific execution, ordering, persistence and return path. The autonomous Ravel manager owns the downstream S3 response to the audit result, not the audit judgment itself. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runVerify in the child&#39;s worktree, configured global/per-repository verify command, captured exit/output verdict, verifying pending set, fail-closed runner failure, persisted DispatchVerification, manager directive ordering and completion hold while verification is pending. - Closure path: child exits and ordinary self-report is captured → first-party Ravel marks verification pending → configured repo verify command runs separately in that child&#39;s worktree → verdict/output is persisted → manager event is withheld until the verdict lands → the manager receives VERIFY COMMAND PASSED/FAILED before the child&#39;s own report and makes the next current-control decision with that contradiction/evidence in context. - Boundary reachability: verification is wired into the normal Ravel child-exit path when the operator/project configures a verify command; no repository-maintainer CI path is required. The first-party runtime deliberately executes it in the finished child&#39;s worktree before invoking the manager on that exit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Conductor establishes a function-specific complementary access and feedback path, but the audit judgment itself is the configured external/deterministic repo command. The manager&#39;s autonomous reaction does not retroactively own that audit judgment. A developer/operator must compose the meaningful verification criterion, so the autonomous audit function remains constructor-owned rather than A. - Evidence: README.md; src/main/ravel.ts; src/main/ravel.integration.test.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the verify command may be a test/build/check script and need not itself be an AI evaluator. That is why the state is C, not A. Human review/landing surfaces provide additional checking but are not used to claim a parent modifier, which Methodology 0.3.x does not publish for S3. - Claim being audited: a child process&#39;s ordinary claim/exit signal that its assigned brief completed successfully and its result is fit to advance the fleet. - Ordinary reporting path: child session exit plus its own report/tail/result enters the Ravel child-exit path. - Complementary access path: separately configured repository verification command executes in the finished child&#39;s actual worktree/branch and produces an independent pass/fail plus captured output. - Independence boundary: the verification process is not the child self-report and runs through a separate first-party hook runner against repository state after the child finishes; runner failure is recorded as failed verification rather than accepted success. - Who acts on findings: the Ravel manager receives the verification verdict before the child&#39;s report and owns the subsequent S3 decision; pending verdicts block premature complete, ensuring the complementary result enters control before closure.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material installation/Ravel-level S4 outside-and-then adaptation loop is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conductor records durable history, computes Insights over current/recent fleet state, supports Roundtable deliberation, schedules future occurrences and can fall back among installed manager harnesses, but these paths do not establish external prospective sensing that develops an adaptation option and returns it into present organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an S4 function at the declared recursion. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic Insights rules, event journal/timeline, Roundtable discussion, cron/heartbeat scheduling, occurrence ledger, installed-harness discovery/fallback and persistent Core state. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: internal performance/coordination/verification observations and scheduled future execution can support operations, but neither is evidence of an externally and prospectively oriented adaptation conversation with S3. - Evidence: README.md; src/main/insights/rules.ts; src/main/insights/engine.ts; src/main/operations/automation-engine.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can ask a Roundtable/Ravel to reason about future strategy, but generic prompt expressiveness is not a packaged S4 organizational loop without a standard environmental sensing/adaptation-return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material Ravel-level identity/ultimate-policy closure is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: users approve exact plan revisions, select mission/model/roster settings, grant shell consent and may stop/detach/review/land work, but these are task/current-control/risk decisions rather than a runtime identity or ultimate-policy issue for the Ravel organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity/ultimate-policy matter. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: exact-plan approval, mission/brief configuration, shell/auto-approve consent, worker confirmation rules, review/land decision path, model/harness selection and operator authentication. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operator has substantial authority, but human final say over a task plan or worker action is not S5 unless the underlying matter concerns identity/ultimate policy and closes that function. - Evidence: README.md; src/main/ravel-model.ts; src/main/operations/worker-controls.ts; src/main/operations/core.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this negative state does not claim the system lacks policy or authority; it keeps present-work approval, permissions and review at their actual operational functions rather than promoting them to S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/78f6d6f954d8e2efe589ee54960ba58e37b16fd6/assessments/reigen.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 78f6d6f954d8e2efe589ee54960ba58e37b16fd6\">78f6d6f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T16:20:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Ryder-Sun/Meldwork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Meldwork</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Ryder-Sun/Meldwork/tree/4c522c0997035af8b931e5420f48b3edc3177a05\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4c522c0997035af8b931e5420f48b3edc3177a05\">4c522c0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce agent-owned task proposals, implementation/analysis artifacts and final synthesis through configured provider-backed participant sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different task requirements, peer objections, tool feedback, partial work and failing provider invocations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: propose a solution, revise a challenge response, choose the substantive content of an owned work artifact and synthesize task evidence. - Decision owner: configured external model-backed participant agents, reached through first-party desktop connector and V4 runner; not the Electron scheduler. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider invocation, persistent snapshot/receipt records, context packs, assigned roles, artifact storage and Human Gate. - Closure path: the participant receives context/peer challenge, returns a decision/artifact receipt, and the V4 discussion runner uses that receipt to advance or require another agent turn. - Boundary reachability: these invocations form the documented supported V4 Auto Discussion path in the installed desktop, not a mock, test or plugin-only example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-specific proposals and artifacts require the configured agents&#39; contextual judgments; deterministic records and queues alone cannot determine their contents. - Evidence: V4 runner proposal/challenge/work, architecture modes and provider boundary. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external providers are optional and need user configuration; no outcome or provider quality is claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: negotiate compatible cross-agent responsibilities before work to reduce ownership conflicts and conflicting plans inside one group. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple agents may propose incompatible task partitions, duplicate responsibilities, or disagree on the finalizer and dependencies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: individually challenge, support or contradict a common plan; collectively admit a single unanimous normalized coordination-plan hash before work starts. - Decision owner: distinct configured proposal/challenge agents; first-party plan validation is deterministic enforcement of their consensus. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: challenge bindings, signed/linked receipts, target-kind membership, common plan hash, assignments, dependency-aware work scheduling and a designated finalizer. - Closure path: each agent receives competing candidate plans and challenges them; unanimous support receipts select the same plan hash; the runner turns agreed assignments into later agent work and repeats challenge when there is no agreement. - Boundary reachability: the V4 Auto Discussion group flow invokes proposal then challenge as normal operating phases and blocks work until a valid coordination plan exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: participant agents own distinct approval/contradiction judgments; code verifies the agreement and changes work allocation but does not choose the substantive partition for them. - Distinct S1 units: agent kinds bound to separate participant slots and challenge receipts. - Inter-S1 disturbance: conflicting planned responsibility/ownership over the shared group task, not merely a transport or message-order concern. - Attenuating coordination relation: unanimous plan support plus allocated owner, dependency and finalizer identities suppress incompatible concurrent work and duplicates within the group. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: accepted responsibilities select subsequent work prompts and the finalizer; disagreement invokes another challenge round. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: independent agent review and a shared plan hash determine who may do what before any work is admitted, actively handling competing plans. - Evidence: coordination unanimity, challenge to work transition. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the controller can still permit separate conversations to collide on disk; S2 here is limited to the declared V4 group and is not global file-lock arbitration.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separately owned whole-group discretionary current-control judgment was established beyond agent-work coordination and deterministic enforcement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: budget exhaustion, failed/late invocations, stalled agents and writable-state ambiguity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous or parent-governed whole-system balancing/commitment choice separate from single-group workflow enforcement established. - Decision owner: none established at this function; operator controls budgets/permissions and scheduler enforces preselected limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-run budgets, invocation watchdog, stop/cancel handling, one conversation-scoped writer and Human Gates for unknown write outcomes. - Closure path: quota checks or user gates stop/retry individual runs; these do not evidence a distinct manager evaluating and reallocating the group&#39;s overall current commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a runner that orders dependencies and consumes receipts is S2/workflow infrastructure, and a human granting permission is not by itself S3. - Evidence: architecture scheduling/Human Gate, V4 phase runner. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a human can pause or reject operations, but no established separately owned whole-group S3 parent decision loop was evidenced.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the synthesized result using primary artifact content and binding evidence instead of trusting the producing finalizer&#39;s completion claim. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a finalizer&#39;s purported output may be unsupported by stored artifact content, lack evidence or conflict with independent reviewer findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a non-writer agent can issue independently attributed review findings and a support or adverse verdict that affects acceptance. - Decision owner: independently invoked configured verification agents, explicitly excluding the synthesis writer; deterministic acceptance only checks their returned evidence and verdicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer binding filtered away from writer, immutable artifact ID/hash, direct stored artifact context, attributed finding records and acceptance guard. - Closure path: the verifier receives the stored synthesis artifact and its digest, records a finding bound to the exact content/run, and a non-support/open finding blocks acceptance and drives another V4 round. - Boundary reachability: V4 uses this non-writer verification path as a first-party production phase in its normal group execution; it does not require test harnesses or manually invented reviewer roles. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a distinct model-backed agent controls the substantive verification finding; content/hash verification by the runner ensures accountability but is not the audit judgment. - Ordinary reporting path: synthesis-finalizer receipt and typed task/artifact claims. - Claim being audited: the designated synthesis writer&#39;s completion claim that its assembled artifact correctly reflects the negotiated work and is evidence-supported. - Complementary access path: another agent reads the stored final artifact and evidence independently of the writer, rather than only repeating its summary or self-reported status. - Independence boundary: V4 excludes the finalizer/writer kind from verifier selection and invokes separately attributed verification agents on the immutable stored artifact rather than accepting a writer self-review. - Independence and returned control: writer-kind exclusion, distinct reviewer attribution, artifact-linked adversarial findings and acceptance veto create an independent corrective return to the V4 controller. - Who acts on findings: the V4 discussion controller rejects acceptance on unsupported/open findings and schedules further corrective rounds; the distinct configured agents own any subsequent revised work. - Evidence: non-writer verifier selection, content-based findings and acceptance, artifact context to verifier, acceptance guard. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: although verification is a normal V4 phase, its distinct source-of-truth access and owner separate it from a mere repeated checklist. Evidence establishes an independent complementary audit path, not that external provider judgments are reliably correct or statistically independent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no durable forward-looking adaptation to a changing external environment established in the supported group runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolution of provider capabilities, task domain and future operating conditions, if an adaptation function existed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate agent-owned or parent-closed environmental scan, option selection and implementation loop found. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configurable providers/connectors, skills, knowledge sources, V4 task proposals and failure policy. - Closure path: changes to a present task&#39;s plan or provider retry can affect current execution, but do not feed a future-facing strategy back into the group identity or operating capabilities. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: proposal/challenge rounds solve the given task, not long-horizon adaptation of the system itself. - Evidence: architecture/deployment boundary, V4 discussion phases. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: future-oriented prompts can be entered by a user, but are not a shipped standing S4 loop for the harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no closed ultimate-purpose/identity dispute and legitimate parent decision loop in a V4 group. - Disturbance / variety regulated: fundamental conflicts about a group&#39;s charter or non-negotiable identity, rather than task solution disagreement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no such legitimate identity-level decision right established inside the shipped desktop runtime. - Decision owner: none established for S5 at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permissions, credentials, Human Gates, trusted Electron IPC and human task goals. - Closure path: an operator&#39;s allowed/denied tool request or a group&#39;s accepted task plan changes operational execution but is not ultimate organizational policy adjudication. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: participant consensus is over a task responsibility graph; the product owner and human user&#39;s task instructions do not establish a separate S5 identity function within the deployed group. - Evidence: architecture and permission boundary, V4 agreed plan schema. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: developer governance and the user&#39;s own policy authority are adjacent actors, not auto-credited as a runtime S5 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e30fcd8b8b92cdd637aa5be0d7e0cd9585931060/assessments/me/meldwork.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e30fcd8b8b92cdd637aa5be0d7e0cd9585931060\">e30fcd8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-03T19:27:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/me7ko-dev/genesis-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Genesis Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-03\">2026-10-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/me7ko-dev/genesis-agent/tree/0e24818a9fd0788c6fe68b2e51ba3a3083f6d2fc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0e24818a9fd0788c6fe68b2e51ba3a3083f6d2fc\">0e24818 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing coding/automation work by interpreting a mission, choosing tool calls or executable code, mutating project/filesystem state, running commands/browser actions/tests and revising based on returned observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous codebases, shell/test failures, web/browser state, provider failures, missing information, execution errors, unsupported completion claims, model/tool formatting failures and implementation alternatives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what action/tool/code to attempt next, interpret returned evidence, change the implementation after failures and decide when to present or submit a candidate result. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Genesis operational actor; delegated autonomous children own the same right for their bounded subtask. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent/tool loop; model router; executor; sandbox; file/shell/browser/research tools; context compaction; repeat guard; budget limits; sessions and memory. - Closure path: goal/current environment → model decision → first-party execution/tool dispatch → observed result/error → next model decision changes subsequent work until a terminal result or escalation. - Boundary reachability: documented terminal, mission and fix commands directly instantiate these first-party loops in the installed distribution; application developers do not need to compose an external agent loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model actor, Genesis retains tools, routing, sandbox and persistence but loses the open-ended substantive judgment selecting and revising coding actions. - Evidence: README.md; genesisagent/agentcore.py; genesisagent/autonomousloop.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference is externally provided, while the credited S1 role/tool/result composition is first-party.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: serialize concurrent persistent-skill registry writes from parallel Genesis S1 workers so their shared capability index is not corrupted by simultaneous updates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independently executing delegated autonomous missions can complete at similar times and both attempt to update the shared skills.json/skill-library state, creating lost-update or corrupted-index interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: admit one skill-persistence transaction at a time to the shared critical section and expose the resulting updated library to subsequent workers/missions. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party lock/runtime machinery. No autonomous actor decides the ordering or collision policy, so this is constructor-owned rather than A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegate.py thread-based autonomous workers; skillsmanager.SAVELOCK; atomic index-file replacement; per-skill slug collision handling; semantic re-indexing after persistence. - Closure path: parallel child S1s independently reach successful skill persistence → each enters saveskill → the first-party lock serializes the shared index update → completed library state is visible to later skill search/reuse and to the other worker after it enters the critical section. - Boundary reachability: both parallel autonomous delegation and the locked saveskill path ship in the installed Python package; no external scheduler or application-specific lock is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the S2 function exists and specifically attenuates a concrete shared-resource conflict, but the deciding mechanism is a fixed mutex/atomic update path rather than an autonomous organizational actor. - Evidence: genesisagent/delegate.py; genesisagent/skillsmanager.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: general parallel delegation/status tracking is not the witness. The positive mapping is narrow: concurrent autonomous workers share a persistent skill registry and first-party serialization exists specifically to prevent their writes from corrupting it. - Distinct S1 units: multiple delegatetask(..., agent=&quot;autonomous&quot;) workers, each running its own autonomous mission loop in a separate thread. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent successful workers may both persist/update the same shared Skills Library index, causing write/write corruption or lost updates. - Attenuating coordination relation: SAVELOCK makes the critical read-modify-write of skills.json single-writer while atomic replacement protects the resulting index file. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: once serialized persistence completes, later skill search/build-context sees the combined library state and may reuse the newly admitted capability. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the lock is explicitly documented and implemented to stop a concrete parallel-write interference at a shared resource reached by independently executing autonomous S1s.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material separate whole-system current-control loop was established at the selected installation/mission recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S3 ownership level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. Genesis has budgets, iteration limits, model escalation, task status, stop events, planners and sequential orchestration, but no first-party actor was found that observes the current multi-S1 organization as a whole and autonomously changes shared commitments/resources/priorities on behalf of that whole. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model/provider routing and escalation; retry/round budgets; delegate task status/waiting; stop event; Planner/Coder/Tester/Reviewer pipeline; repeat guards. - Closure path: not applicable; the located mechanisms either regulate one operational loop, execute a prearranged sequence or enforce fixed constraints rather than close a distinct whole-current organizational decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operational writer self-corrects and a critic can reject a result, but neither path supplies the required separate current-control authority over the entire live organization. - Evidence: genesisagent/autonomousloop.py; genesisagent/orchestrator.py; genesisagent/delegate.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this does not deny useful control logic inside one mission; it applies the Profile distinction between S1 self-regulation / deterministic lifecycle machinery and a distinct S3 whole-current function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge an operational writer&#39;s claim that generated code fully accomplishes the mission before the result is accepted and promoted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: generated code may execute cleanly and even pass a narrow self-test while still omitting a mission requirement, performing the wrong side effect or otherwise producing a false-positive success claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue a semantic YES or NO: &lt;reason&gt; judgment over the mission goal, candidate code and observed code output after the deterministic verifier has passed. - Decision owner: a separate model-backed critic invocation; the mission code explicitly excludes the writer&#39;s current provider/model pair from the critic call to reduce same-model self-approval. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sandbox execution; verifyskill; non-tautological self-test detection; provider/model avoidance; quality-failure accounting; retry/escalation loop. - Closure path: writer produces candidate → executor and verifier establish runnable/self-test evidence → separate critic reads goal/code/output and judges semantic completion → NO becomes corrective feedback to the writer and forces another round; only verifier + critic acceptance reaches persistent skill promotion/success. - Boundary reachability: this critic is wired directly into the documented genesis mission autonomous loop in the shipped package, not a CI/test-only reviewer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the decisive semantic completeness judgment is produced by a separate autonomous model call. Deterministic verifier machinery supports access/evidence, while the critic owns the open-ended judgment about whether the result actually satisfies the goal. - Evidence: genesisagent/autonomousloop.py; genesisagent/verifier.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary inline self-tests alone are not credited as S3. The positive mapping depends on the distinct semantic critic after objective execution/self-test evidence and its explicit writer-model separation. - Claim being audited: that the writer&#39;s generated program fully accomplished the specific mission goal, not merely that the program ran. - Ordinary reporting path: the operational model produces code; Genesis executes it and obtains stdout/stderr plus verifier status. - Complementary access path: a new critic conversation receives the original goal, actual code output and candidate code after execution, with no writer conversation history required. - Independence boundary: the critic is a separate model invocation and brain.complete(..., avoid=writerpair) prevents it from using the same provider/model pair that authored the candidate. - Who acts on findings: Genesis mission runtime returns a critic NO reason to the writer as a new correction instruction; only YES permits final skill persistence and mission success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: turn evidence from completed and failed missions into persistent future capability/behavior changes that are automatically reintroduced into later Genesis operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future missions may repeat previously solved implementation work or recurring failure patterns, wasting inference and reproducing known mistakes as tasks/environments recur. - Decisive decision or feedback right: generate a reusable solution during operation, semantically approve it through the independent critic, and thereby permit promotion into the persistent verified skill repertoire; failed mission evidence is also categorized into reusable future guidance. - Decision owner: the model-backed operational writer plus separate autonomous critic close the substantive skill-adaptation judgment without a required human approval step. Deterministic verifier/persistence/indexing and deterministic lesson distillation enforce/transport that decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: saveskill; sandbox verification metadata; semantic skill indexing; Brain.buildcontext; reflection.recordmission, distilllessons and lessonsforprompt; episodic memory. - Closure path: current mission/environment produces success/failure evidence → writer creates a candidate solution and independent critic determines semantic fitness → successful candidate is persisted/indexed as a skill while failures are recorded/distilled → a later mission&#39;s buildcontext / system prompt retrieves verified skill code and lessons → subsequent operation is changed by the learned capability/guidance. - Boundary reachability: autonomous mission execution, automatic post-success skill persistence and future Brain.buildcontext reuse are all standard first-party runtime paths documented in README and wired into the package. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reusable capability content and semantic acceptance are autonomous model decisions. The filesystem/index/reflection mechanisms do not invent the adaptation; they make the accepted adaptation durable and return it to later work. - Evidence: README.md; genesisagent/autonomousloop.py; genesisagent/skillsmanager.py; genesisagent/brain.py; genesisagent/reflection.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary conversation/session memory is not the witness. The credited S4 loop uses audited mission evidence to mutate a persistent skill/lesson layer that is automatically consumed by later missions. - External distinction: concrete mission/environment evidence reveals which generated solution actually works and which failure patterns recur under real execution rather than model expectation alone. - Future / prospective distinction: promoted skills and distilled lessons are stored specifically beyond the current mission and searched/injected when later goals are processed. - Adaptation option generated: model-generated, verified and critic-approved executable skill code; additionally, recurring failure categories produce distilled future behavioral guidance. - Path back into current capability / S3: saveskill writes/indexes the accepted skill; later Brain.buildcontext searches verified skills and injects their code for direct reuse/extension, while lessonsforprompt changes later mission system prompts.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity/ultimate-policy resolution loop was established at the selected Genesis installation recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. Genesis DNA constants, operator identity configuration, strict-authority checks, red-zone tokens, sandbox policy and safety classification govern or constrain execution, but no first-party loop was found in which an identity/ultimate-policy issue is deliberated by a legitimate S5 owner and a new authoritative identity/policy decision is returned into later operation. - Decision owner: not established as an S5 function. Static developers/configuration and the local operator supply constraints/credentials outside the qualifying runtime loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Genesis DNA constants; GENESISOPERATOR; strict-authority gate; red-zone token/secret; sandbox SAFE/CONFIRM/BLOCKED classification; user confirmations. - Closure path: not applicable; the reviewed paths enforce standing rules or one-off operational permission rather than close an identity/ultimate-policy issue → authoritative decision → returned governance loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operational agent cannot revise the non-negotiable DNA/ultimate policy through a first-party S5 process; configured operator authority and red-zone confirmation are enforcement/authorization controls, not sufficient identity governance under Methodology 0.3.6. - Evidence: genesisagent/dna.py; README.md; genesisagent/sandbox.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the project uses identity/policy vocabulary such as DNA and sovereign operator, but names and standing constraints are not enough to infer S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/76e6d7c52598a1d8674b37541bc88fb7c905288b/assessments/genesis-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 76e6d7c52598a1d8674b37541bc88fb7c905288b\">76e6d7c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T00:55:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/liumaishenjian/codej\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CodeJ</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/liumaishenjian/codej/tree/e51e6b5062fbab46881137342a2bff84bfc05645\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e51e6b5062fbab46881137342a2bff84bfc05645\">e51e6b5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute and adapt coding, investigation, workspace modification, command/test, and correction work against a user&#39;s task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: codebase uncertainty, differing file content, failing tool results, command errors, and changing user goals within the allowed session. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select a subsequent coding/tool action after model turns and observed workspace/tool responses. - Decision owner: the root model-backed AgentRuntime, and the separate child model-backed AgentRuntime for delegated subtasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool Pipeline, permission/approval/sandbox, provider adapter, session journal, context handling, and command/file tools. - Closure path: user/child objective → model next action → pipeline workspace/command tool → returned evidence → subsequent model decision or terminal outcome. - Boundary reachability: standard headless composition registers the first-party tools and owns an ordinary AgentRuntime; the same runtime is instantiated for children. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic host tools cannot decide which code investigation or correction best pursues a task without a model-backed operational choice. - Evidence: AgentRuntime.java; HeadlessRuntimeSession.java; DelegateAgentTool.java. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: assessed by frozen first-party source and script/fake tests; no live LLM was invoked. The model&#39;s choices remain within the permission and execution policy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate competing work claims and inconsistent task/dependency transitions among separately executing S1 child/root actors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two child agents attempting the same Task; stale owner/claim epochs; contradictory dependency and progress mutations; resuming an interrupted actor&#39;s claim. - Distinct S1 units: a root coding AgentRuntime and up to four concurrent supervised child AgentRuntime instances, each with independent Sessions, model turns and bounded tool scopes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: top-level children can share a root-owned Task Board over host-approved task IDs; competing claims, writes to the same task state and stale revisions can collide even where their coding worktrees are separate. - Attenuating coordination relation: the board serializes mutation, enforces CAS/claim epochs, actor capabilities, dependency DAG and blocked-by checks; a contested claim is rejected with explicit diagnostics rather than accepted as two independent owners. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: taskupdate and tasklist/get return the current authoritative claim/status and conflict to the model, which can choose another task, change dependencies or defer work; successful mutation changes later agents&#39; view. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the exclusive claims and dependency/conflict results regulate demonstrable collision between concurrently acting S1s; supervisor queue limits and worktree creation alone would not establish S2. - Decisive decision or feedback right: resolve which task to claim or reassign after live competition feedback, with atomic admissibility enforced by the host. - Decision owner: root/child model actors select work and react to conflicts; the Java Task Board owns deterministic acceptance/denial of mutations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskListService, TaskUpdateTool, ChildTaskBoardAccess, TaskBoardCapabilityFactory, AgentSupervisor, per-child actor identity and durable Task events. - Closure path: multiple S1s request overlapping claims → synchronized claim/CAS check → one success or conflict return → agents observe revised task state and adjust subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: HeadlessRuntimeSession.initializeTaskBoard() exposes model Task tools; childTaskBoardAccess() and HeadlessChildRuntimeScopeFactory bind a top-level child to the same parent board and narrow allowed task IDs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: host CAS itself cannot decide the next meaningful work; model actors retain task-selection and recovery decisions in response to the returned coordination signal. - Evidence: TaskListService.java; TaskUpdateTool.java; HeadlessRuntimeSession.java; HeadlessChildTaskBoardCompositionTest.java. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: shared task scope is opt-in in a top-level delegation, and the Task Board does not provide cross-worktree semantic merge or universal file locks. In unscoped child runs, this S2 path may not be active.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: observe and regulate the current root Session&#39;s task allocation, active progress, dependencies, child delegation and immediate execution exceptions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unbalanced/blocked task progress, unfinished Plan steps, stale ownership, failed children and changed execution outcomes requiring present-time reallocation. - Whole-system current view: root-scoped tasklist/get sees the root&#39;s canonical task graph and statuses; AgentSupervisor reports child terminal status, failures and summaries to its parent, while the TUI projects the same board state. This is the bounded current organization, not all unrelated CodeJ processes. - Current-control decision scope: the root model creates tasks and dependencies, chooses delegates and task scopes, revises assignments/status, and can issue later corrective actions; approved-Plan execution couples those transitions to actual terminal control. The host applies capability/approval gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the current work split and revise current task execution in view of reported progress, blocked dependencies and failures. - Decision owner: primary root model-backed actor in normal operation (A); in the alternate user-controlled Plan path the human owns Plan approval/re-approval and may prevent or authorize present execution (P). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskListTool, TaskCreateTool, TaskUpdateTool, DelegateAgentTool, AgentSupervisor, Plan coordinator, session store and TUI progress/approval panels. - Closure path: current task graph and child reports → root model&#39;s revised task/delegation decision → host-checked mutation or child dispatch → updated board/child evidence → next present-time control decision. In Plan-parent mode, user approval/re-approval gates the corresponding execution Run. - Boundary reachability: root Task tools and delegation are registered in shipped Headless composition; child status/journal return, Plan approval/resume and TUI Board are ordinary product paths rather than separate maintainer scripts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the default root model chooses priority, task breakdown and active correction; deterministic queues/revisions only implement bounds. Human Plan approval is recorded as a different owner, not silently attributed to the model. - Evidence: HeadlessRuntimeSession.java; AgentSupervisor.java; TaskListService.java; TaskToolProductionCompositionTest.java. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: root task board does not prove general multi-team enterprise control; child process cancellation is exposed separately through host/TUI APIs rather than established as a root model Tool. Parent approval is a bounded operating mode, not an organizational S5.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently test specified evidence supporting an approved Plan&#39;s claimed completion, and refuse to promote an unsupported final answer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementer model can write convincing completion prose even if a required workspace file is missing/changed, a verification Tool failed/never ran, or a Plan Task remains incomplete. - Claim being audited: final AssistantMessage proposing completion of an already approved Plan execution, distinct from the human&#39;s initial decision to approve the Plan. - Ordinary reporting path: model final answer, self-updated task statuses, and declared results in the implementing loop. - Complementary access path: a host-owned PlanExecutionCorrectionController reads the durable Plan Task cohort and verifies actual workspace files by guarded re-read/SHA-256 plus recorded successful tool-result events directly from the Session. It does not rely on model prose that a check passed. - Independence boundary: an enforced final-handler gate runs on the host side, checks underlying workspace/journal facts against existing requirements, and can withhold a model-proposed final answer. It is bounded to approved-Plan evidence types and shares the product host; no separate autonomous investigator, independent audit policy or general audit sampling was found. - Who acts on findings: host requests up to two correction turns; recurring or unrepaired failures stop the Run with PLANVERIFICATIONREQUIRED, preserving durable NEEDSVERIFICATION. A human can later explicitly reapprove verification resumption. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept or reject declared completion based on direct evidence rather than implementer self-report, within an externally supplied Plan brief. - Decision owner: host-side validator judges specified facts and enforces an override; the human supplies approval/review authority; the model does not own independent audit strategy. This supports only a bounded constructor (C). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: validatePlanEvidence, PlanExecutionCorrectionController, durable PlanArtifact/Ledger, guarded filesystem, tool-result journal, Plan Task snapshots, and FinalAssistantDecision. - Closure path: model final claim → separate direct-evidence recheck → failed witness rejects final and provides correction details → resumed agent work or durable blocked terminal state. - Boundary reachability: Plan execution wires the final-handler into the ordinary headless/TUI Run path; offline first-party tests demonstrate bounded rejection and durable state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic evidence validation has material separation from model claims, but cannot invent and execute independent audit scope on its own; the host and user retain the decisive gate. - Evidence: HeadlessRuntimeSession.java; DurablePlanExecutionHandoffTest.java; PlanEvidenceLedger.java. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: AutoReviewCoordinator reviews permission ASK before operations, not completed work, so it is not additional S3 evidence. The validator only checks a prescribed file or successful tool event, not semantic correctness of code or correctness of an arbitrary LLM conclusion.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party future/external-facing organizational adaptation decision with a return to CodeJ&#39;s current whole-runtime control was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: web search, model routing, skills/plugins, context memory, long-running task planning, provider retries and benchmark outputs can inform a user task, but do not constitute a distinct prospective self-adaptation system. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for prospective capability or environmental repositioning of the operating CodeJ organization. - Decision owner: none established; the user chooses configured providers/plugins, the model chooses task-level approaches, and maintainers evolve CodeJ through external development. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web tools, context projection/compaction, file memory, provider router, staged roadmap, project feature matrix and G0–G6 regression/evaluation harness. - Closure path: no autonomous outside/future diagnosis → strategic alternative → authorized present-system adaptation return loop found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: operational research/planning and retrospective benchmark reports do not by themselves create a long-term agent-owned adaptive organization. - Evidence: README.md; docs/reference-architecture.md; ContextPreparationService.java; ProviderRouter.java. - Basis: explicit + bounded structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the project itself has an extensive future roadmap, but it is not runtime S4 of the assessed user-facing agent system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct operating-level identity, ultimate-purpose or policy-adjudication process returning binding decisions to the same runtime organization was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, approvals, hard denials, session retention, governance configuration, sandbox requirements, and root-owner Plan approvals are concrete operational constraints, not evidence of ultimate constitutional policy judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party right to reconsider CodeJ&#39;s identity or arbitrate S3/S4 purpose tension is implemented. - Decision owner: users/hosts set tool permissions and approved plans; developers configure managed feature policies; none is a shown identity-level systemic S5 feedback owner within the product. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionPolicy, DefaultHardDenialPolicy, AutoReviewCoordinator, Plan approval, ManagedGovernance, and SDK AgentControlApi.governance() read-only projection. - Closure path: no identity/purpose dilemma → legitimate final authority → binding identity-policy return path into the evaluated root Session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a permission classifier approves particular tool requests, while the SDK&#39;s governance endpoint describes enabled feature gates; neither sets organizational self-identity or adjudicates its purposes. - Evidence: AutoReviewCoordinator.java; AgentControlApi.java; ManagedGovernance.java. - Basis: explicit + bounded structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the repository&#39;s term governance names managed configuration/provenance; terminology cannot substitute for an operational S5 function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/41c8758ba9006c81ccefbe4604b77b3cc1abe5a3/assessments/co/codej.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 41c8758ba9006c81ccefbe4604b77b3cc1abe5a3\">41c8758 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-15T05:03:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/fwtllh-png/QCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">QCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-15\">2026-10-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/fwtllh-png/QCode/tree/0807662a3bd41efe4e7fdf74b3d260971d9e1cf0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0807662a3bd41efe4e7fdf74b3d260971d9e1cf0\">0807662 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: adaptively carry out one software-engineering task through model-guided repository tool execution and observation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code structure, failing tests, code edits, model and tool outcomes, permission or sandbox denials, changing workspace state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next repository search/edit/test/tool action and revise it from execution outcome. - Decision owner: parent or child model actor in first-party internal/runtime/agent/engine executing a supported live coding turn; provider servers calculate inference but not the first-party agent execution organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model and tool adapters, permission/policy/constitution guard, workspace journal, sandbox, persistent conversation/evidence, user approval gates. - Closure path: input/task → parent engine model call → selected guarded coding tool → filesystem/process feedback and journal/verify receipts → engine feeding results → subsequent model-driven action or completion. - Boundary reachability: qcode shipped main runtime and guarded coding tools operate with the explicitly configured provider/workspace on macOS, without borrowing another coding-agent CLI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model makes the substantive choice of tool and follow-up based on observed task; deterministic gates can veto or constrain but do not choose the semantic coding solution. - Evidence: README.md; internal/runtime/agent/engine/engine.go; internal/runtime/agent/engine/turnhandler.go; internal/adapter/tool/agent/agent.go. - Basis: explicit first-party source. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: unsupported platforms, provider absence and deterministic operations alone are not independent S1=A evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate actual inter-worker interference from concurrent editing of overlapping files before parent integration, and use conflict feedback for subsequent S1 work partition/revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent coding child S1s independently edit the same paths or supply incompatible changes, making merges/parent workspace inconsistent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: parent model scopes eligible independent child tasks, examines real path-conflict reports and integration candidates, chooses follow-up, rerouting, redo/discard or candidate integration; mechanical worktree separation prevents premature shared mutation. - Decision owner: model-driven parent in supported first-party adaptive delegation mode; role/policy constraints and host journal enforce, but do not make semantic conflict decisions for the parent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated child worktrees, per-agent path claims, Result.Unresolved conflicts, preview digests and stale-plan rejection, guarded merge rollback, mailboxes/list/wait/followup/integrate tools. - Closure path: parent model scopes/spawns distinct child agents → worktrees isolate concurrent writing → child terminal write claims report overlap/incompatibility → model-visible outcomes and preview influence parent integration/reassignment → guarded merge/followup changes subsequent coding work. - Boundary reachability: subagent manager, adaptive delegation policy and all model-visible child/integration tools are shipped under QCode&#39;s own runtime, not an uninstantiated external framework. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: worktree/claim/merge checks are deterministic; the model parent must resolve which conflicting candidate or path to adopt and what to re-run. Removing the model owner leaves diagnostics and a protection gate, not the task-specific conflict-resolution return. - Distinct S1 units: separately running model-guided child coding agents with thread/context, tool authority and worktree isolation, under one parent session. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two independently generated edits to the same path; changed-file conflicts and stale integration candidates after parallel work. - Attenuating coordination relation: scoped delegation plus isolated execution, first-party cross-child write claim visibility and digest-bound preview/apply under the parent-directed integration right. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Result.Unresolved/worktree merge preview/child state evidence is reported to the parent and allows subsequent model-controlled followup and integration choices, with accepted merge changing shared workspace. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: an actual overlapping-write conflict is recorded at cross-agent ownership level and accounted for before code enters the shared parent workspace; adaptive remediation is exposed to the parent, rather than merely queuing unrelated tasks. - Evidence: internal/orchestration/subagent/claims.go; internal/orchestration/subagent/result.go; internal/orchestration/subagent/integration.go; internal/orchestration/chatmerge/service.go; internal/adapter/tool/agent/agent.go; internal/orchestration/subagent/controlplane.go. - Basis: explicit source and mode-specific structural ownership. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: unlike Foe&#39;s pre-admission directory exclusivity, QCode allows conflicting isolated work to finish and reports it before integration. Positive S2 relies on model-owned parent choice/feedback, not conflict reporting alone.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise current parent-session team commitments and resource limits, choosing allocation/intervention among subordinate ongoing coding units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child/task overcommitment, budget/concurrency saturation, stalled or failing children, stale integrations and unfinished delegated work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: parent model inspects current child roster/status/results, chooses eligible child spawning/followup/interruption/closure and integration priority in response to actual current obligations. - Decision owner: model parent, with initial delegation permission bounded by user/developer/skill in explicit mode and a first-party adaptive-delegation mode when supported/configured; runtime governor accounts and enforces tokens/cost/depth/concurrency but does not choose semantic current task commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentControl/manager&#39;s full child graph, listagents/waitagent, child status and receipts, admission governor Snapshot, persistent session, child worktree and process controls. - Closure path: live team/session child statuses and results → parent model chooses followup/spawn/interrupt/close/integrate → runtime governed action updates child/team state and shared workspace → later parent model observations reflect changed current commitments. - Boundary reachability: first-party child agent control and parent model-visible tools are packaged in shipped executable and can be used in supported adaptive mode with configured models and workspace. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: governor constraints and manager state transitions follow fixed code, while model parent decides what part of ongoing team work to prioritize or adjust. A human clicking Web controls is not silently credited as autonomous S3. - Whole-system current view: one current session&#39;s subagent graph, statuses, ownership, direct receipts and unresolved work visible via listagents/control. The assessment makes no claim of one global QCode supervisor governing all user Workspaces as a model-owned organization. - Current-control decision scope: task delegation and limits within available capabilities, child turn followups, shutdown/intervention, selection/discard/application of child work, next coding commitments. - Evidence: internal/adapter/tool/agent/agent.go; internal/orchestration/subagent/subagent.go; internal/orchestration/admission/governor.go; internal/orchestration/subagent/controlplane.go; README.md. - Basis: source/structural owner return. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: positive S3 is at parent-session recursion and does not imply global fleet-level policy, shared human governance or full automatic load optimization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: possible task-independent read-only reviewer or specialized verifier inspection of coding claims against direct evidence, with findings returned into parent correction before integration; whether this is a closed complementary independent organizational audit rather than task-time QA is unresolved in the shipped modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agent implementation claims differing from actual patches/tests, misleading self-reported child results, unsafe changes from a subordinate episode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independently tasked reviewer/verifier would inspect artifact/diff/test evidence, issue an independent judgment and cause a binding parent/operation corrective response. - Decision owner: RoleReview and RoleVerifier are separately instantiated, read-only/test-focused child agents when selected, but no mandatory distinct audit authority or returned corrective decision path is proven from role catalog alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in role contracts, child evidence/verification receipts, review and verify tools, worktree diff visibility and parent integration preflight. - Closure path: read-only review child can send findings to parent in a supported composition; inspected source does not establish that the separate complementary judgment is authoritatively consumed to re-regulate parent S3 rather than optionally considered as normal task QA. Thus positive and bounded absence are both withheld. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: child reviewer is an agent capable of separate analysis, but the required independent evidence boundary and corrective-return ownership have not been reconstructed for the shipped ordinary operational integration path. - Evidence: internal/orchestration/subagent/controlplane.go; internal/orchestration/subagent/result.go; internal/orchestration/subagent/integration.go; internal/adapter/tool/agent/agent.go; README.md. - Basis: unknown following inspection of concrete first-party plausible path. - Confidence: unresolved. - Caveats: check whether a supported role/test/approval flow provides an independently attested verdict and obliges parent rework or blocks integration; naming a child review is insufficient.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no evidenced first-party sensing of future external ecosystem change that generates and applies strategic/capability adaptation for the QCode organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new external threats/requirements, prospective future coding tools/behaviors, future needed organizational capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: assess future-environment distinctions, develop adaptation options, select one and close return into current QCode operation. - Decision owner: human maintainers/operator choose feature/config/skill adjustments; QCode agent plans steps for current task but does not own a shipped strategy adaptation loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan and context modes, sessions, skills, MCP extensions, repo indexing, environment metadata, telemetry and roadmap. - Closure path: persisted context or a user-installed skill can alter future requests, but no shipped autonomous external sensing → option evaluation → capability/config change → subsequent operational feedback is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: future-looking code edits when requested by a human are ordinary task S1; plugin/skill extensibility is not agent-owned prospective organizational intelligence. - Evidence: internal/runtime/agent/engine/engine.go; internal/runtime/agent/engine/turnhandler.go; README.md; docs/zh-CN/architecture.md. - Basis: structural bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: user-authored skills or future release work may add adaptation but are not credited at the pinned standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: permission/policy/constitution/approval enforce developer/operator-selected safety boundaries, not a first-party ultimate organizational values/identity resolution loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool execution risk, unsafe write changes, sensitive credentials, approval requests and child delegation overreach. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimately resolve a disputed ultimate organizational policy/identity matter and return the authoritative determination to following operations. - Decision owner: human operator configures policy and approves ordinary risky actions, while runtime protects invariant restrictions; child agent has bounded delegated authority. The model does not own ultimate goals. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: constitution deny-write and tool scope, OS sandbox, postures never/suggest/auto, policy/permissions, append-only journals, user prompt and tool gate. - Closure path: configured constraint → guarded operation allow/deny/ask → code runs or blocks. Ordinary approval updates a tool decision, not system ultimate-purpose governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: hard safety enforcement and even mandatory approval cannot by itself decide identity-level tradeoffs; standalone P is not awarded from a human clicking an approval popup. - Evidence: README.md; internal/security/constitution/constitution.go; internal/orchestration/subagent/controlplane.go; internal/adapter/tool/agent/agent.go; internal/security/policy/policy.go. - Basis: bounded source negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an organization deploying QCode might build parent policy governance externally; this is not credited to the first-party runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6ccb23bbc8a29753d59a1c547aba8812367ff9fa/assessments/qcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6ccb23bbc8a29753d59a1c547aba8812367ff9fa\">6ccb23b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T00:44:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jonathanung/strike\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Strike</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jonathanung/strike/tree/0191bb896b5a1969c5b7b784597ed891b814234a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0191bb896b5a1969c5b7b784597ed891b814234a\">0191bb8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform coding, repository inspection, editing, shell/build/test work and local task-specific recovery against a changing workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous requirements, differing code paths, changing file state, failed tool/command results, and model/tool uncertainties while pursuing assigned outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task-specific next model/tool action and revise the sequence after returned tool, workspace or child evidence. - Decision owner: lead model-backed session, or each child model-backed session for its own bounded operational objective. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Go engine loop, tool registry, permissions, file-state guards, session JSONL, budgets, scheduler, optional sandbox and provider adapters. - Closure path: user/subtask objective → model request/decision → first-party tool action → returned result and updated session state → later model decision or completion. - Boundary reachability: standard CLI/headless/engine composition builds and invokes this loop; child creation uses the same operational engine. No evaluation-only or contributor-only actor is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves a deterministic tool runner and control plumbing but no context-dependent choice among code edits, investigations and validations. - Evidence: harness/engine/engine.go; cmd/strike/assembletools.go; harness/engine/child.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: no live model was invoked during this source-pinned assessment; tool permissions may block a chosen operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate conflicting work claims and path modifications among separately acting child/lead S1 sessions in one shared workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two live agents can claim the same work item, overwrite an actively held file/prefix, or submit mutually incompatible patches. - Distinct S1 units: lead and spawned child sessions with separate engine/model/tool action loops, and multiple concurrent children on the same implicit team. - Inter-S1 disturbance: live team members share a workspace and team task/patch state; duplicate task claims and conflicting file leases/touches are explicitly represented and rejected or warned by first-party code. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-facing teamtask uses owner-exclusive claim and CAS; agentownership supplies inspectable exclusive/shared leases; write touches and patchcollab expose overlaps, with session.overlapPolicy=block able to reject conflicting mutation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: conflict/claim results return to the invoking model, live path overlaps reach the team event stream, and ownership claims alter what other member sessions may subsequently claim/write under the configured policy. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: claim exclusivity and conflict-aware path leases target demonstrable shared-workspace collision modes of independent S1s rather than merely transferring or listing their tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise task claims, leases and coordinated file scopes in light of contested ownership and overlap feedback. - Decision owner: model-backed team actors choose claims/scopes and adjust behavior; deterministic shared board, path tracker, CAS and policy enforce or report conflicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Team shared state, team board, PathOwnership, agentownership, policy setting, file mutation hooks, patch conflict detection and event delivery. - Closure path: autonomous S1 requests claim/lease → conflict-aware team registry decides admissibility → return result/warning/block → same or competing S1 chooses a different task/path or waits; subsequent changes obey the updated claim. - Boundary reachability: cmd/strike/assembletools.go registers teamtask, agentownership and patchcollab in the shipped registry, while the standard child engine shares the team&#39;s state and file ownership tracker. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: code can reject a preset conflict, but without an autonomous actor it cannot decide which meaningful task/file scope should be taken next following a denial. - Evidence: harness/engine/teamboard.go; harness/tool/ownership.go; harness/tool/agentownership.go; harness/engine/teamcontroltest.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the default overlap policy warns rather than blocks file writes; stronger protection is a supported configuration. The claim board itself nevertheless enforces exclusive ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current subordinate commitments, resources, priorities and interventions on behalf of a live multi-child session team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independently evolving child work becomes blocked, stalled, over-budget, conflicting or obsolete relative to the lead&#39;s overall objective and remaining resources. - Whole-system current view: the model-facing agentroster exposes the complete session team with current status, objectives, last actions, blocks, touched files and budget/usage/stall fields; the progressive task list/status API tracks a shared delegation population, not just a single awaited handoff. - Current-control decision scope: lead can choose child assignments/personas/budgets and dependencies, subscribe to block/completion feedback, interrupt/cancel/reopen work and transition owned delegations; supported root human controls can separately spawn, interrupt and change delegation/board commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: revise live-team resource and commitment choices using current population observations, including exceptions and blocked/dependency state. - Decision owner: autonomous lead model in the base mode; human operator controlling the live root team via the shipped web-cockpit protocol in the distinct parent mode. Neither the scheduler nor the dashboard owns these choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: roster and task tools; child state/budget record; delegation lifecycle with CAS and dependency guards; shared scheduler; human team. Op permission/identity/root checks and browser UI. - Closure path: live team roster/delegation exception → lead model observes status and issues bounded task create/cancel/transition or allocation update → engine changes child commitments and reports state → continued child/lead action. In parent mode, live team observation → human operation through team.spawn, team.childinterrupt, team.tasktransition, or board Ops → engine applies/rejects and publishes updated team state. - Boundary reachability: the CLI registry exposes roster and progressive task controls to the lead; the first-party live web cockpit implements separate root-authorized human team-control Ops through the same engine, not an imagined plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the lead model from the autonomous mode leaves a status dashboard and rule enforcement but no task-specific cross-child intervention judgment. Parent mode makes that judgment explicitly human, with the same first-party return path. - Evidence: harness/engine/agentroster.go; harness/tool/task.go; harness/engine/delegation.go; harness/engine/teamcontrol.go; internal/frontend/server/api.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: claims concern one active session team; global scheduler limit config is enforcement, not agent-owned budget governance. The human path requires an active, mutation-authorized live root; historical/attach-only and read-only modes do not close it.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: offer a parent/host-controlled, implementer-independent completion challenge on a subordinate actor&#39;s claimed result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementer can wrongly claim to have completed required work or tests; routine model handoff text may assert success without supporting reality. - Claim being audited: a child session&#39;s completed/self-reported verification and, optionally, a solo root&#39;s claimed final turn. - Ordinary reporting path: model-produced terminal handoff (summary, verification, files changed and findings) or ordinary tool results in the implementer&#39;s execution stream. - Complementary access path: a separately executing engine verify.Runner inspects actual filesystem path existence, independent shell-command outcomes and/or structured handoff schema, ignoring the model&#39;s self-certification string; a failed check changes a claimed-completed child to blocked and injects failures into handoff blockers/lead notification. - Independence boundary: the checks run after the child has claimed success under the parent engine, not as the child&#39;s own verification prose. However the shipped general component is a configured deterministic gate runner; its independent audit strategy, sampling, or model-based auditor is not a fully autonomous, separately assigned first-party role. - Who acts on findings: the parent engine blocks promotion of a failed child and feeds gate failures to the lead; the lead or operator can correct/re-delegate. Autonomous complementary audit judgment beyond the specified gates must be composed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which independent checks to require and interpret their direct-evidence failure, versus accepting the implementer claim. - Decision owner: the parent/host configuring verify gates owns the audit brief; the independent deterministic runner owns factual pass/fail evaluation and blocking, not autonomous audit policy or judgment in the general case. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: harness/verify/verify.go, harness/engine/verify.go, child.go gate-to-blocked transition, verification events and handoff error propagation. - Closure path: implementer completion claim → independent parent-side gate execution → direct-evidence verdict → failed claim demoted to blocked and exposed to lead → bounded corrective response/redelegation. Constructor state reflects missing autonomous auditor/selection ownership rather than absence of this first-party function-specific path. - Boundary reachability: the shipping task API accepts verify gates, child.go invokes the independent runner, and the ordinary parent receives blocked/status evidence; this is not imported from the separate benchmark evaluator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no model can override a failed gate by claiming it passed; the shipped checking judgment is deterministic and task-configured. The runtime offers the independent complementary witness, but does not itself provide an autonomous audit investigator beyond it. - Evidence: harness/verify/verify.go; harness/engine/verify.go; harness/engine/child.go; harness/engine/verifygatestest.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: opt-in gates cover defined outcomes and can resemble ordinary QA; C is credited only to the bounded first-party complementary check of implementer claims, not to evaluation/CI in the adjacent development organization or a claim of broad independent review.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct externally and prospectively oriented adaptation system that changes Strike&#39;s own present organizational capability was established at the assessed team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external web/documentation tools, code intelligence, agent/persona selection, repository memory, plan/workflow/goal loops and model routing can influence current work, but the reviewed paths do not form a prospective capability-adaptation conversation with S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for future environmental adaptation of the operating organization. - Decision owner: not established; agents can plan user tasks and operators can change static config, not thereby own S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web/browser tools, goal-loop planner/critic, scheduled loops, workflow phases, skill/persona discovery, model routing and local memory/ledger. - Closure path: no first-party external/future distinction → developed adaptation option → capability intervention returning into team-wide current S3 control was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current task research, local replanning, recurring prompts and failure memory remain current operations without first-party prospective strategic choice/return. - Evidence: docs/goal.md; docs/loop.md; docs/agents-skills.md. - Basis: explicit + bounded structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: users can script ongoing market or technology research with tools, but that is not a first-party always-linked adaptation decision in the assessed organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision and returned governance loop exists at the assessed runtime team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission, sandbox, provider selection, workflow gates and human approval constrain tools and phases, but do not themselves adjudicate organizational identity or an S3–S4 policy tension. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no such ultimate-policy issue/decision right established at the team boundary. - Decision owner: developers/operators select settings and grant ordinary access; they do not constitute a documented first-party runtime S5 closure merely by doing so. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: managed permission denies, tool authorization, sandbox, config, team. human control, audit log, workflow /autonomy gate. - Closure path: no identity-level issue → legitimate ultimate authority → binding policy return into the same operating team established. Human team. acts on live work and is counted only as S3 parent control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither model nor operator is given a first-party process for resolving ultimate identity/policy questions; existing policies merely bound choices already assigned elsewhere. - Evidence: harness/engine/teamcontrol.go; docs/human-orchestration-ops.md; docs/usage.md. - Basis: explicit + bounded structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: higher-level real human or OSS governance may exist outside this operational recursion, but co-location is not a first-party S5 product path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8d7a754a51eda30f9474bbce8d8d67dbcb56abd1/assessments/st/strike.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8d7a754a51eda30f9474bbce8d8d67dbcb56abd1\">8d7a754 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-28T16:51:52Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJU-REAL/HugAgentOS\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HugAgentOS</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-28\">2026-09-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJU-REAL/HugAgentOS/tree/9436fcb77261bc995e6e7a45cdc5e192eae9bc74\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9436fcb77261bc995e6e7a45cdc5e192eae9bc74\">9436fcb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform substantive project work through model-driven iterative reasoning, tool use and observation-driven continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous tasks, changing project/file state, tool/service results, browser/command outcomes, model uncertainty and evolving user context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task-specific reasoning/tool actions and revise subsequent actions from returned observations until the bounded outcome is complete. - Decision owner: the model-driven ReAct actor assembled and executed through HugAgentOS&#39;s first-party runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: orchestration/workflow.py, core/llm/agentfactory.py, AgentScope integration, tool registry, sandbox/MCP execution, project/context/memory injection, streaming and approval controls. - Closure path: project objective + assembled context → model actor selects action/tool → HugAgentOS executes within the permitted boundary → result returns to the actor → later model action changes in response → project outcome advances. - Boundary reachability: ordinary first-party project-chat and supported background paths instantiate the ReAct runtime directly; no contributor-only actor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor while retaining FastAPI, tools, persistence and sandbox leaves execution infrastructure but removes the task-specific discretionary action choice. - Evidence: document/en/modules/chat.md; src/backend/orchestration/workflow.py; src/backend/core/llm/agentfactory.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: AgentScope and model/provider internals remain dependencies. A credits the model actor reached through HugAgentOS&#39;s supported first-party harness path, not organizational functions internal to those dependencies.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate a concrete runtime interference mode between concurrently dispatched S1-capable sub-agents by isolating each child&#39;s asynchronous execution/cancellation scope. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple child agents using MCP/AnyIO machinery can otherwise create cross-task cancellation-scope errors that disrupt sibling operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine the execution-isolation boundary for each dispatched child so one sub-agent&#39;s async/cancellation lifecycle cannot corrupt another child&#39;s task scope. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party HugAgentOS runtime policy in callsubagent; no autonomous coordination actor chooses this isolation relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated ThreadPoolExecutor, one event loop per child thread, child cancellation bridge, isolated MCP clients and separated child runtime construction. - Closure path: parent dispatches distinct S1-capable children → unsafe cross-task async interference is separated by per-child thread/event-loop isolation → each child continues its own model/tool lifecycle → independent results return for subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: callsubagent is a shipped first-party tool and the isolation is its ordinary implementation at the pinned revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the anti-interference policy remains if the model agents are replaced; deterministic runtime code decides and enforces the isolation boundary. The function is therefore constructor/runtime-owned C, not A. - Distinct S1 units: two or more concurrently dispatched model-driven child/main agents capable of substantive delegated project work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: cross-task AnyIO/MCP cancellation-scope interaction can raise runtime errors and interrupt sibling child execution when async lifecycles share an unsafe task boundary. - Attenuating coordination relation: each child executes in its own worker thread with its own event loop and isolated MCP/client lifecycle, keeping cancellation scopes inside the owning task. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: isolated children remain able to execute later model/tool steps and return results instead of failing from sibling cancellation-scope corruption. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping rests on an explicitly named cross-task failure mode and a first-party isolation mechanism intended to remove that interference. Delegation, routing and the pool are not independently treated as S2. - Evidence: src/backend/core/llm/subagenttool.py; document/en/modules/chat.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a narrow technical coordination function. It does not imply that all sub-agent routing, batching or delegation is S2.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-project current-control function is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: individual autonomous loops regulate requirement progress, retries, checkpoints, reviewer findings, stalls and hard limits; automation/batch paths regulate their own jobs. These are lower-scope execution controls rather than a distinct project-wide S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying whole-project current-control decision over active operational resources, commitments, priorities, constraints or synergy is established. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: autonomous-loop ledger, one-requirement-at-a-time driver, checkpoints, replan hooks, runaway breakers, background lifecycle, batch orchestration and user approvals. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planner/worker/reviewer agents make bounded task decisions while deterministic drivers enforce one loop/job&#39;s progression. Neither demonstrates a separate whole-project S3 owner. - Evidence: document/en/modules/autonomous-loop.md; src/backend/orchestration/autonomousloop.py; src/backend/orchestration/batchorchestrator.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: at a narrower recursion one autonomous-loop driver is a strong controller of its own worker/reviewer process; this assessment does not lift that local task controller into project-level S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently verify whether a worker&#39;s claimed autonomous-loop requirement is actually implemented by inspecting real project output through a separate read-only agent path, then return findings into correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: worker self-report can claim completion when underlying files/output do not satisfy the requirement; the ordinary worker path can therefore hide defects or absent deliverables. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect actual produced files/command evidence independently and return done, continue, offtrack or needhuman for the requirement. - Decision owner: the separate model-driven loop reviewer agent instantiated by the first-party autonomous-loop driver. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct reviewer context, read-only capability policy, same project sandbox, explicit acceptance criteria, structured verdict/evidence, independent second pass, sub-agent logs and deterministic driver routing. - Closure path: worker produces candidate + summary → driver starts independent reviewer with requirement/criteria but not worker conversation → reviewer reads/searches/runs read-only checks against actual project output → verdict/findings return to driver → non-done findings change subsequent work; accepted evidence permits ledger advancement. - Boundary reachability: autonomousloop.py invokes reviewrequirement in the shipped autonomous-loop path; docs expose the loop through the normal API/frontend and canrunautonomousloop capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the substantive audit judgment is produced by a distinct model-driven reviewer. Deterministic read-only restrictions and driver routing enforce the boundary but do not decide whether the output satisfies the requirement. - Claim being audited: the worker&#39;s claim that the current requirement is complete and its project artifacts satisfy the requirement/acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: worker summary and its ordinary model/tool trajectory, plus machine evidence produced during the work iteration. - Complementary access path: separate reviewer agent personally opens/searches the same project sandbox and may run read-only commands rather than trusting worker self-report. - Independence boundary: reviewer and worker use distinct contexts/agent executions; the reviewer does not reuse the worker conversation, is explicitly read-only and is deterministically spawned by the driver rather than by the worker being judged. - Who acts on findings: the autonomous-loop driver; continue/offtrack/needhuman feed correction/replanning/escalation, while only accepted evidence-backed completion advances the requirement ledger. - Evidence: document/en/modules/autonomous-loop.md; src/backend/orchestration/autonomousloop.py; src/backend/orchestration/subagents/loopreviewer.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ontology committee/review code is not needed for this finding; the credited path is the directly reachable autonomous-loop reviewer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established at the assessed project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: HugAgentOS preserves memory, observes tools/services and can adapt private capability from accumulated operational episodes, but the reviewed path does not maintain a distinct model of the outside/future environment and return adaptation options through a separate current/future organizational conversation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying S4 prospective adaptation judgment/feedback right is established. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project/user memory, automation/triggers, personal evolution, historical-episode mining, semantic SOP judge, private skill candidates and owner approval. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: personal evolution genuinely changes capability, but it mines the user&#39;s own historical operating episodes for repeated successful patterns. Internal learning from past operations is not enough by itself to establish Profile S4. - Evidence: src/backend/core/evolution/personal.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the negative finding distinguishes real history-driven capability adaptation from the specific outside-and-then organizational function required for S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain the durable project identity and ultimate operating rules that define project goals/scope, authoritative sources, work methods, output/quality expectations and operating boundaries for later project operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project drift, missing or obsolete durable rules, conflicting/incomplete operating instructions and changes to project goals, quality requirements or boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the authoritative root AGENTS.md content for the project and make that policy decision govern subsequent project-agent operation. - Decision owner: Base (A) mode — the model-driven project-init agent decides the exact rule update after inspecting current rules and representative project material, then persists and verifies it. Parent (P) mode — a legitimate project editor/admin directly edits the same authoritative root file through supported project-instruction surfaces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ProjectInstructionsService, project permissions, optimistic revision checks, project-bound instruction tools, runtime approval mode, context assembly and project system-prompt injection. - Closure path: identity/policy issue → project-init agent synthesis or legitimate parent edit → authoritative root AGENTS.md write → later project context reads changed instructions → project system prompt injects them → subsequent project-agent behavior is governed by the returned policy. - Boundary reachability: project instructions, /init, direct authorized instruction editing and automatic project-context injection are shipped Community Edition paths at the frozen revision. The documented auto and full approval presets provide supported write-permitted modes in which ordinary project-instruction persistence does not require a human to choose or approve the exact model-generated content. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in base mode, the operator selects the initialization operation but the model actor makes the semantic decision about which durable goals/workflows/quality/boundary rules to add or revise from project evidence. Removing the model leaves storage and permission machinery but not that project-policy judgment. In parent mode, the authorized human directly owns the authoritative policy edit. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what the project is for and the durable rules later project agents must follow, including goals/scope, important sources, workflows, output/quality expectations and operating boundaries. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Base (A) — the project-init model actor for the exact synthesized update in the autonomous initialization mode; Parent (P) — the authorized project editor/admin for direct authoritative edits. - Return-to-operation path: changed root AGENTS.md is the canonical project-instruction source, is loaded into project context, and is injected into subsequent project system prompts/conversations. - Evidence: document/en/modules/projects-myspace.md; src/backend/core/services/projectinstructions.py; src/backend/core/llm/tools/projectinstructionstool.py; src/backend/prompts/projectinit.md; src/backend/prompts/projectsection.py; src/backend/core/llm/agentfactory.py; document/en/modules/chat.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: /init is explicitly user-invoked and is instructed to preserve human-authored constraints. A credits the model-owned semantic project-policy decision within that supported mode; it does not imply authority to erase higher-level operator constraints or rewrite unrelated platform policy.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f53e2824a5e4f85955b21cb94f5d22f1ef4aad21/assessments/hugagentos.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f53e2824a5e4f85955b21cb94f5d22f1ef4aad21\">f53e282 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T17:56:07Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/brenonaraujo/git-meta-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">git-meta-harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/brenonaraujo/git-meta-harness/tree/167b13c66993fa0a5216e4be2fc9bf911cd02cf8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 167b13c66993fa0a5216e4be2fc9bf911cd02cf8\">167b13c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Independently implement or inspect product changes through distinct model-backed builder, architect and specialist personas, in contact with real issue requirements and repository work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar user requirements, changing source code, failing tool/build responses and task-specific choices about edits or investigations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Installed persona model acting via Hermes selects the content of source edits/technical output and returns a comment, PR, revised specification or real artifact to the project. - Decision owner: Project-specific model-backed worker personas materialized by gmh seed and launched through supported Hermes profile/runtime; external runtime supplies terminal/LLM execution but role-specific mission/contract is first-party. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persona Markdown, Hermes cron, spawn-persona.sh, issue/PR state, executable specialist commands, branch rules, local Git repository, deterministic CI/sensor gates. - Closure path: Project requirement → manager-dispatched persona issue/brief → worker chooses and executes terminal/source operations → comments/branch/PR/test outputs → returned GitHub state for subsequent work/next manager tick. - Boundary reachability: Source-owned seed.Apply writes persona and spawn contracts, loop installer schedules recurring manager launches, team-manager-tick.md calls spawn-persona.sh with a real named profile; this is an executable first-party installed operating path, not just a sample infographic. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The installed personas are explicit external-model-powered actors, and the content of implementation/technical judgments is not determined by a static issue-label script. Hermes owns tools, not the whole delivery governance. - Evidence: seed.go L1-145, spawn-tm.sh L1-24, spawn-persona.sh L1-23, team-manager.md L15-88, team-manager-tick.md L10-32. - Basis: Model-backed first-party materialized actor plus usable task/feedback path. - Confidence: Medium-high source-structural; live external-model session not run here. - Caveats: Merely executing gmh seed without Hermes gateway/provider/GitHub authorization does not produce completed S1 tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent destructive interference among independently running builder S1s that would otherwise edit overlapping source paths on parallel sub-issues. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Simultaneous frontend/backend and same-layer builders can overwrite or break shared files/types and lose incompatible in-flight work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The materialized safety check compares actual path-scope/dependency metadata across candidate tasks, blocks uncontrolled overlaps and requires an explicit dependency/serialization or scope adjustment before dispatch. - Decision owner: The project actor/team manager and author of path-scope/dependency labels choose how to refactor/serialize; first-party sensor computes and enforces the specific interference check. No independent fully autonomous S2 agent decides compromise. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: path-scope: and depends-on: issue labels, check-parallel-builders.sh Python glob overlap analyzer, Sensor 10 and manager predispatch policy. - Closure path: Multiple ready/in-progress S1 builders → conflict in intended changed file scopes detected → fail/blocked state on missing/disputed scope → manager revises mutual scope/dependency or serializes → safe future builder launches. - Boundary reachability: Sensor 10&#39;s executable shell script calls GitHub to inspect actual issue labels and exits with blocking status for an uncovered overlap, while the installed manager&#39;s persona contract requires this check before issuing parallel work. Still requires the installed operator/manager to invoke and heed the gate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Detection is deterministic; the decisive policy/negotiation over which parallel work to delay is supplied by the project manager/label owners. Source provides a specific S2 constructor/enforcement path, not autonomous ownership. - Distinct S1 units: Independently spawned backend/frontend or multiple builder personas on separate live sub-issues/branches. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concrete concurrent edit overlap in paths/definitions (reported as root cause in the first-party Sensor 10 design note; not independently re-enacted). - Attenuating coordination relation: Block on non-empty path-scope intersection without declared depends-on, and force disjoint file scopes or explicit serial dependency before the manager starts workers. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Failed executable check prevents uncontrolled concurrent in-progress launches; manager must change dependency/path assignments before resuming builder operations. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: It detects a particular inter-builder source-file interference condition and specifically changes mutual scheduling; not simply following an issue DAG. - Evidence: check-parallel-builders.sh L1-210, 10-decomposition-safety.md L1-128, team-manager-tick.md L16-32. - Basis: Shipped context-dependent inter-S1 conflict detector and constructor routing; incomplete autonomous compromise ownership. - Confidence: Medium source-structural; glob overlap is heuristic and actual live gh queries were not reproduced. - Caveats: Missing gh or jq fails the check; manager must actually invoke the sensor in its installed mode. A comment-only division without enforceable predispatch use would not by itself establish S2.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Govern the current delivery project across ongoing independent specialist work cells by tracking the whole issue/PR state and making present assignments, commitment/intervention and closure decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting current priorities, stalled domain refinement, dead builders, unreviewed PRs, rejected QA, dependencies blocking delivery, excessive parallel workers and missing human validation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured model-backed team-manager reads open GitHub Issues/PRs at every cron tick, selects issue type/persona/branch, moves labels, limits worker concurrency, prompts failed/rejected work, defers blockers and determines when the project task is eligible for final delivery/human validation. - Decision owner: First-party materialized Hermes team-manager model-backed actor, separate from coding-worker personas and provided explicit team-wide current-delivery governance mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: gmh seed persona contracts, Hermes cron schedule, GitHub issue state, labels, branches, gh CLI, spawn-tm.sh, spawn-persona.sh, max-three-workers guard, QA and scope sensors. - Closure path: Whole current issue/PR/blocked/dependency/QA board → model manager chooses updated assignment/constraint/repair → changes GitHub label/branch or spawns named persona → subsequent specialist starts/stops/repairs work → manager sees next returned state and may adjust further. - Boundary reachability: loop.CronCommands creates recurring no-agent launcher of live model-manager plus orchestrator supervisor; installed scripts and materialized role source require actual GitHub state reads and actions, not merely a hand-designed static workflow graph. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A bare scheduler periodically launches the manager; the manager, not cron, exercises discretionary current staffing/recovery choices and holds the whole-board view. Removing that actor leaves no contextual policy owner for resolving delivery commitments. - Whole-system current view: gh issue list --state open, gh pr list --state open, issue labels/dependencies, QC/CI, waiting/failed workers and progress across the installed project&#39;s specialist cells. - Current-control decision scope: Decide if/which persona works next, maintain max three simultaneous workers, create shared branch, move commitments through states, block or repair stalled work, send QA rejection back to builders and gate closure. - Evidence: loop.go L103-164, spawn-tm.sh L1-24, team-manager-tick.md L1-32, team-manager.md L1-160, 07-hermes-loop.md L1-92. - Basis: Model-backed current whole-project regulator with evidence-returning resource and commitment effects. - Confidence: Medium-high source-structural, not a claim of user project&#39;s real deployment metrics. - Caveats: The authored role mandate alone is not sufficient; the shipped cron/launcher and tools close its reachability. Source does not prove the manager always obeys the prompt or that a real external Hermes gateway was run in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently challenge builders&#39;/manager&#39;s ordinary claims of completed work with raw source/CI/tests/acceptance evidence and route failures to current managerial correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Builders may say pronto despite failing checks, security holes, missing contracts, visual regressions or incomplete DoD. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Distinct configured model-backed quality-assurance persona reviews the actual PR/DoD, tests/sensors and optional live snapshot, reports APROVADO versus REPROVADO and places the issue in qa or back in in-review. - Decision owner: QA model persona separate from implementing builder and issue-wide team-manager; GitHub CI/sensors provide independent observable facts, not autonomous audit judgment on their own. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: QA persona, source PR diff, DoD issued by architect, static/security/contract/smoke/snapshot sensors, GitHub comments/labels, next manager cron tick. - Closure path: Builder PR/current completion claim → QA independently loads DoD and actual source/tests/CI → failed finding plus report/label in-review → model manager notices returned state and spawns builder for repair → new PR status/review. Pass returns qa, leading to human acceptance/merge control. - Boundary reachability: team-manager-tick.md explicitly spawns QA when PR+CI is ready and explicitly spawns a builder on rejected QA; quality-assurance.md defines executable commands, code/DoD inspection and label-producing return actions. Applies in the materialized first-party project flow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separately prompted QA specialist reads evidence rather than trusting the builder summary, forms an accept/reject judgment, and triggers corrective action through a distinct manager. Sensor exit codes are supporting evidence only. - Claim being audited: A coding persona&#39;s claimed implementation/PR readiness and fit to architecture-defined DoD. - Ordinary reporting path: Builder&#39;s branch/PR, completion comment, issue label in-progress/in-review and claimed tests. - Complementary access path: QA accesses raw branch/diff, code tests/security/contract/smoke checks and possible actual running snapshot independent of the implementer report. - Independence boundary: Distinct persona and process dispatched after builder, separate QA report; may share Hermes model vendor/infrastructure, so not vendor-independent. - Who acts on findings: Team-manager reassigns the builder on a rejected QA result, withholds merge pending green checks and/or human acceptance. - Evidence: quality-assurance.md L1-160, team-manager-tick.md L17-32, 02-pull-request.md L41-133. - Basis: Independent source/acceptance observation with active corrective return to current management. - Confidence: Medium-high conditional on configured Hermes and project test tools. - Caveats: Source does not demonstrate that listed external snapshot/Gatling/tool commands are installed in every adopter; source-specific QA decision and repair return, not every illustrative test, support the function.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Form future-oriented persona/skill improvement options from previously observed external project issue/comment evidence, with a first-party prescribed handoff but no self-contained completed autonomous application. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeated missing acceptance criteria, weak DoD, domain persona gaps, missing skills and changing project delivery needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic evolve.Propose selects issue-comment snippets by keyword and drafts suggested persona/skill patch prompts; a separate model-backed Hermes manager could choose actual changes and a human must explicitly validate before modifications. No in-product autonomous change/commit decision is made by this CLI. - Decision owner: CLI&#39;s keyword extraction and prospective proposal scaffold is first-party; decisive editing/adoption remains with external composed Hermes actor plus human approval. Hence C and not A, A(P) or P. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Issue/comment JSON fixtures, harness/memory/traces/&lt;utc&gt;, deterministic proposal.md, PROMPT.md, (--apply) only persistent trace write and explicit human validation instructions. - Closure path: Historical issue/PR comments → deterministic keyword/signal extraction → written prospective persona/skill adjustment proposal.md → human/Hermes handoff in PROMPT.md → possible approved persona/skill update → next project&#39;s persona execution. Return is documented as a required external composition, not executed within gmh evolve. - Boundary reachability: Shipped gmh evolve --from-dir ... --apply creates concrete trace and change prompt artifacts and existing harness/workflow/08-persona-evolve.md specifies who may act on them; however this command intentionally does not rewrite persona files and the model runtime/approval handoff is not natively executed, so no autonomous S4 closure is claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Keyword matching and prompt text generation are deterministic, not an agent choosing/accepting new future capability. The installed manager could respond to prompt only as separately composed external agent/user behaviour. - External distinction: GitHub issue/comment records reflect actual project/domain gaps and user acceptance problems from previous delivery, not an internal same-action memory reset. - Future / prospective distinction: Proposed edits target the persona/skill library for later issues, rather than simply retrying the current PR. - Adaptation option generated: Suggested changes to skill/persona instructions derived from historical AC/DoD/persona gap references, recorded for later human/editor decisions. - Path back into current capability / S3: PROMPT.md → external Hermes manager → required human validado → changes in harness/personas/ or harness/skills/ → future manager/builder ticks. This path is specified but not source-executed, a concrete constructor gap preventing A. - Evidence: evolve.go L1-187, evolve.go L1-92, 08-persona-evolve.md L45-112, EVOLVE docs. - Basis: First-party future-capability proposal and explicit constructible parent/agent-return path, not closed autonomous adoption. - Confidence: High on deterministic source limits, medium on hypothesized later handoff. - Caveats: --apply only writes traces and prompts. A README claim of evolving personas is not evidence they were changed; the human approval contract should not be counted as executed action.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No established legitimate ultimate organizational purpose/identity decision over the delivery organization is made and returned through this first-party harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requirements, project-specific persona names, GitHub merge protections and ordinary human acceptance can limit delivery but do not necessarily present a contested constitutional purpose/identity decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human specifies project requirements and may validate release/persona edits, while software enforces workflow stages; no first-party S5 actor legitimately resolves whole-system purpose conflicts and returns a revised ultimate policy. - Decision owner: The product adopter&#39;s true organizational leadership is outside the scoped generated delivery system, while team-manager&#39;s authority is current delivery S3 rather than supreme mission-setting S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: docs/SPEC.md, PROJECT.md, harness/AGENTS.md, GitHub PR protection, validado instruction and CLI persona domain policies. - Closure path: User spec / final acceptance → current delivery limits; missing legitimate identity/purpose conflict → terminal rightful decision → revised system-wide organizational identity applied to future operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Team manager routes development work; human PR approval and static constraints do not adjudicate legitimacy or constitutional identity simply because they can veto a merge. - Evidence: seed.go L1-145, team-manager.md L1-85, 08-persona-evolve.md L68-112, 02-pull-request.md L85-120. - Basis: Source-first bounded absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A customer organization may exercise real S5 elsewhere. Its existence does not flow into this product merely because someone provided a product spec.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e25bd40f14ddace94dea2df5d190b5a86b61edac/assessments/gi/git-meta-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e25bd40f14ddace94dea2df5d190b5a86b61edac\">e25bd40 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T19:58:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/savant0x/savant-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Savant-Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/savant0x/savant-code/tree/dbe8b40559e7a455db21b904e512f96b454bdab3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dbe8b40559e7a455db21b904e512f96b454bdab3\">dbe8b40 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Implement user-scoped coding tasks by choosing source inspections, edits, commands and subsequent steps in response to tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed source files, unknown code paths, build and test failures, tool errors, model responses and requested task changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The main or delegated model selects tools and arguments for its task, observes executed results and chooses next actions or final output. - Decision owner: Model-backed Savant main agent (HYBRID) and routed specialist models (STRICT/delegations) instantiated by the shipped agent runner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry, filesystem safeguards, model adapters, context buffers, evidence ledger, endturn gate and optional FID enforcement. - Closure path: User goal/current messages → model request → first-party tool execution → response/result and repository feedback → updated model message/history and subsequent action. - Boundary reachability: The published CLI enters run-agent-step and executable tool handlers with first-party agent templates; installed model providers supply tokens but do not replace the first-party tool/action feedback path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent chooses task-specific operations; EHEL validates and blocks prohibited effects rather than generating those implementation decisions. - Evidence: run-agent-step and loop, tool dispatcher, README runtime contract. - Basis: first-party model/tool code and documented shipped entrypoints. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Provider account/weights, actual coding accuracy and live LLM outcomes were not tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate concrete interference among multiple FID task lanes working from one source repository. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Independent task worktrees could mutate the same checkout, cross-use code graph state or merge incompatible/stale changes into main. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The host creates registered isolated FID worktrees and refuses stale, dirty, nonconverged, conflicting or wrong-branch merges; no autonomous agent owns merge-conflict resolution across the teams. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic FID worktree lifecycle/merge primitive under operator/model-issued action; developer/operator chooses how to resolve a refused merge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cross-process lock, per-FID branch/worktree record, Git merge-tree dry-run, exact base SHA, clean-tree checks, graph snapshot/refresh and refusal reasons. - Closure path: Concurrent FID teams → distinct managed repository lanes → specialized operations on lane-specific source → attempted merge checked against main and either fast-forwarded or explicitly refused with conflict/divergence feedback → operator/team must adjust before subsequent write/integration. - Boundary reachability: The shipped fidworktree host tool routes create/merge/list/dispose through runFidWorktreeAction and mergeFidWorktree; it is an optional supported workspace mode, not a test-only helper. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: It is a first-party function-specific inter-team interference barrier and feedback path (C); deterministic refusal and human/model repair after a conflict do not constitute autonomous S2 arbitration (A). - Distinct S1 units: Two or more FID coding-agent sessions operating in parallel lane worktrees, each making coding decisions for its assigned FID. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Cross-team shared-filesystem writes and incompatible lane changes reaching a common main or graph state. - Attenuating coordination relation: Registered lane identities, cross-process lifecycle lock, protected base/ref checks, conflict-only refusal and independent worktree environments. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A denied merge identifies the exact blocked condition; the lane remains isolated while an operator or coding agent repairs/rebases instead of silently contaminating main. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The worktree and merge guard explicitly prevent cross-S1 modification/commit interference; mere task delegation or ECHO phase order is not treated as S2. - Evidence: FID worktree service, refusal-only merge, merge tests. - Basis: explicit function-specific optional runtime and targeted test sources. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Conflict resolution is explicitly not automated. No S2 claim is drawn from deterministic ECHO phase handoffs among roles of one task.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Maintain task-wide present coordination over active coding/review specialists and required evidence/quality commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unresolved task scope, competing immediate implementation priorities, failed review, incomplete FID evidence and blocked phase transitions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The main orchestrating model chooses coding strategy, whether to write directly or delegate, which specialist to call, what follow-up to request from review, and when to request completion; guards may reject the proposed decision. - Decision owner: First-party main Savant Orchestrator model with specialist routing capabilities; phase/evidence engines are transport and constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent definitions, allowed tool/spawn lists, FID phase gates, EHEL write/refusal checks, outcome messages, Auto Drive phase directives and loop guards. - Closure path: Task goal and live tool/FID evidence → Orchestrator model&#39;s decomposition/delegation/correction → specialist operations and adversarial verdicts → updated work/current commitments or completion. - Boundary reachability: The shipped model/tool runtime registers spawnagents and spawnagentinline and supplies an actual Orchestrator prompt/role; the optional STRICT role chain is explicitly selectable, while the declared-only event orchestration preset contributes no extra owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The LLM chooses real work priorities, handoffs and responses to findings; deterministic strict gates constrain legality and protect evidence rather than deciding substantive work strategy. - Whole-system current view: Current user goal, conversation, selected execution mode, tool state/results, active FID and role phase, returned reports, audit findings and context/evidence state for the local task team. - Current-control decision scope: Task decomposition, delegated work and reviewer feedback, immediate repair/re-analysis/closure commitments; not across all unrelated installed Savant user workspaces. - Evidence: main runtime step, spawn handler, phase driver, README HYBRID/STRICT. - Basis: first-party runtime model authority plus bounded phase-return contract. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A generic static event preset in protocol.config.yaml is declaration-only. Auto Drive deterministic FID selection does not itself own agentic S3 judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Challenge code-change and ordinary implementer/Verifier quality claims with independently observed evidence and return actionable findings into current work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsupported success reports, fabricated or stale line citations, missed high-risk checks and self-review blindness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A fresh Verifier model renders evidence-bounded PASS/FAIL/NEEDS-REVIEW findings, while a separately spawned Adversary model independently resolves sources and may override Verifier findings; both findings are bound and returned to the task control/FID phase. - Decision owner: Distinct first-party Verifier and Adversary agent roles backed by model calls; the Adversary&#39;s contradictory judgment is specifically a complementary audit rather than an inline deterministic permission gate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Verifier zero-tool restriction, raw-evidence injection, Adversary read/search-only tools, spawnagentinline/spawnagents verdict capture, FID phase validation, optional signed provenance bindings and mandatory no-write permission boundaries. - Closure path: Forge/main change and claimed test/grep evidence → fresh Verifier independently assesses provided raw context → Adversary reads real source to re-check and can refute/confirm → verdict attached to task/FID review context → Orchestrator revises work/phase or records unresolved NEEDS-REVIEW. - Boundary reachability: agents/verifier/verifier.ts and agents/adversary/adversary.ts are shipped named agent definitions; live spawn handlers parse their verdicts and the STRICT mode contracts request both roles after implementation. This does not rely on offline benchmark judges. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The auditor models choose which claims pass or fail and the fresh Adversary has its own source-evidence search right; deterministic binding and evidence adapters enforce that output but do not predetermine the verdict. - Claim being audited: Code change meets the converged FID/user requirements, the tests/checks actually ran, and cited source bytes support implementer and Verifier claims. - Ordinary reporting path: Forge/main agent change summary and its tool outputs return to Orchestrator. - Complementary access path: Verifier receives raw change/test/graph evidence outside ordinary reports; Adversary independently opens source and call sites with read-only tools to test Verifier citations. - Independence boundary: New specialist model invocations have distinct agent templates/tools and cannot write; Verifier is zero-tool, Adversary&#39;s independent reads are not the implementing agent&#39;s own self-report. Optional ZTAP receipts attest role separation mechanically, not epistemic truth. - Who acts on findings: The main Orchestrator via current rework/phase choice and FID evidence gates. - Evidence: Verifier role, Adversary role, spawn verdict binding, provenance verdict. - Basis: distinct shipped role contracts, source-evidence tools and live verdict return. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: This is a supported strict-mode autonomous audit, not proof every HYBRID file edit always invokes the pair; agent review accuracy was not empirically measured.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Use cross-session operational failures to propose and selectively adopt future tool-use/recovery skills for later coding-agent work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeated failure classes and erosion of skill utility observed across sessions; unreviewed skill drafts can degrade future operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Mechanical recurrence and Scribe/skill authoring generate candidates, but the human workspace operator alone makes the live-skill trust/reject/rollback judgment; quarantined output is not available to active agents without that decision. - Decision owner: Legitimate local parent operator through the shipped /skills show &lt;name&gt; --review and /skills trust &lt;name&gt; CLI workflow (and related untrust/rollback); autonomous models may propose but cannot authorize release. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PostToolUseFailure capture, cross-session dedup/14-day threshold, SessionEnd agenda, lesson-to-skill drafting, isolated skill-proposal quarantine and lifecycle ledger, version/diff/trigger-case gates and live skill loader exclusion. - Closure path: Tool failure traces across sessions → repeated pattern / durable lesson candidate → quarantined skill draft → operator inspects exact draft and required trigger cases → operator trustSkill promotes to canonical live skill → next agent session can discover/load new skill, altering usable present capability. - Boundary reachability: protocol.config.yaml declares the first-party failure/SessionEnd hooks; published Bun scripts generate proposals, the CLI dispatches the actual operator-only trust route into trustSkill, and skill loader excludes drafts until it is run. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is a closed parent-governed adaptation mode (P). Autonomous draft generation is not autonomous S4 ownership because no agent has the decisive promotion right; a one-click full automatic adaptation path is not claimed. - External distinction: Changes in observed tool/provider/workspace failure signatures across different previous coding sessions, represented as classified and deduplicated experience records. - Future / prospective distinction: A recovery technique to be available for similar future failures, subject to the existing live-skill baseline and regression/trigger-case review. - Adaptation option generated: Proposed SKILL.md draft or revision/FID from recurrent tool failures, held in quarantine. - Path back into current capability / S3: Operator trust changes the canonical .agents/skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md accessible to later skill loading and hence the Orchestrator&#39;s future task capability. - Evidence: self-improving harness guide, SessionEnd capture/review, lesson candidate/draft, CLI trust decision, live trust transition, loader guard. - Basis: concrete parent decision and shipped capability return with independent historical signal and draft construction. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: lessons:to-skills and skills:evolve are invoked as documented commands, not guaranteed on every SessionEnd. skills:evolve only creates proposals and does not touch live skills; the trust path requires valid skill trigger cases and explicit operator action. No runtime evidence of a successful real-world adaptation was collected.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No complete identity/ultimate-policy issue-to-legitimate-authority-to-governing-return loop in the local assessed organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ECHO process violations and risky commands threaten operational safety; that is not sufficient evidence for a constitutional identity dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party S5-specific organizational purpose or ultimate identity judgment is wired into task execution. - Decision owner: Not established at this recursion. Operator controls trust, model and mode settings, but those are operational/adaptation decisions, not shown to be S5 governance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static 15 ECHO laws, user approvals, EHEL enforcement and break-glass flags, model/provider selection and skill immutability. - Closure path: Approval or mode changes can affect task rights, but no evidenced higher-level identity/policy issue → parent constitutional decision → changed organizational charter/current action path is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fixed engineering policy and role prompts are design constraints, not a live agent or parent identity adjudicator. - Evidence: ECHO protocol document, EHEL enforcement, runtime config. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: The broader OSS project has maintainer governance, but a written constitution or user-facing risk approval alone does not demonstrate function-specific S5 closure at the local workspace boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/463ed28830946b957cafcbb035631614efda7229/assessments/sa/savant-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 463ed28830946b957cafcbb035631614efda7229\">463ed28 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T03:39:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/samansarmasik-alt/forcecode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ForceCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/samansarmasik-alt/forcecode/tree/518672e170f570f59c2d2abdeb4b5cf148e1603b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 518672e170f570f59c2d2abdeb4b5cf148e1603b\">518672e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform uncertain coding tasks with model-selected repository inspection, editing, verification and repair actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing code requirements, project-specific tools, failed commands, missing artifacts and model/provider interruptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The active coding model or each independent read-only teammate chooses its next task-level tool action based on observed result. - Decision owner: First-party Agent model loop and child models, with deterministic tool/sandbox enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkspaceTools, ForceSandbox, model/provider interface, project artifact receipts, retry policy, process/session controls and task queue. - Closure path: Developer objective → AI tool/plan step → real file/shell/test result → observed evidence returned → next model decision or verified completion. - Boundary reachability: The shipped Force and forcecode.py entrypoint invokes the normal Agent; --team/VibeCode instantiate the same first-party model/tool operating loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses situational coding operations; static test gates cannot decide which change to make. - Evidence: Agent and managed-team run; execution/kernel; supported CLI. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate concrete concurrent-claim and shared-board state collisions between independently running worker terminals in the shipped terminal fleet. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two worker processes or actor threads could otherwise simultaneously read/update shared worker queues and reports, lose one another&#39;s updates or duplicate a claimed assignment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Native exclusive file lock and atomic queue pop allow only one claimant/board mutation at a time; a worker receives only its claimed task and other terminals see updated state. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party queue/board primitives, not an autonomous actor choosing adaptive cooperation policy. The broader coordination response requires a composed manager/operator: C, not A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TerminalFleet.filelock, activetask/queue status, TeamBoard.lock and publish, shared report context and manager barrier. - Closure path: Concurrent attempts to mutate board or claim task → serialized lock/claim → one valid task assignment/update published → workers subsequently see distinct queued tasks/current reports rather than duplicate/lost state. - Boundary reachability: Persistent terminal fleet is an explicit built-in /terminal and worker mode; manager and workers share these first-party records with real worker claim/publish calls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: File/queue locking and task claims apply fixed concurrency constraints; model discretion is for task assignment and report integration, not the lock arbitration. - Distinct S1 units: Separate model-backed worker terminals, each with its own read-only operative loop and queued assignments in persistent fleet mode. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent worker attempts to claim/edit shared queue/board state and overlapping assignment acquisition. - Attenuating coordination relation: Exclusive file lock during queue pop/report publish with one activetask per worker, plus synchronized manager phase/report board. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Each terminal executes only its claimed task; next claim observes queue after prior mutation; manager sees updated reports for integration. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The mechanism actively prevents lost-update and duplicate-claim interference across concurrent first-party workers rather than merely transporting messages. - Evidence: TerminalFleet.claim/publish and TeamBoard locks; fleet mode documentation. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Exercise whole-current managerial authority over live specialist workforce, assignment priorities, report integration and corrective action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple worker roles/queued jobs/results need coherent present commitments; integrated changes must be prioritized, verified or redone, and worker lifecycle may need adjustment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In autonomous managed-team mode the model selects distinct specialist assignments from the objective, observes shared reports at the barrier, then decides concrete integration edits and verification. In parent mode the operator sees fleet status and uses /terminal add/task/configure/remove to change current live commitments. - Decision owner: Root manager model for A mode; legitimate terminal operator for separate P mode. TeamBoard/TerminalFleet enforce state changes but do not make discretionary whole-team judgments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamBoard phase/reports, rundelegations supervisor, manager-only integration pass, TerminalFleet running roles/queues/states, dedicated /terminal commands. - Closure path: Manager views assignment plan and results → modifies worker commitments or integrates/fixes/validates current work → subsequent source state or worker tasks change; operator actions similarly mutate live fleet queues and active membership. - Boundary reachability: Built-in --team and /team managed mode and /terminal persistent fleet; model fleet action is explicitly user-authorized, and same first-party registry implements human command mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model chooses current workforce assignments and integrated corrective action in one legitimate mode; parent can independently exercise current whole-team control in a separate supported mode, so A(P), not automatic S3 in solo turns. - Whole-system current view: Shared TeamBoard assignments/reports/phase plus TerminalFleet activetask, pending queues, current worker PID/status/report records. - Current-control decision scope: Allocation of specialist responsibilities and current worker resources, termination/reassignment, multi-worker integration and verification of collective outcome. - Evidence: runmanagedteam, TeamBoard, TerminalFleet; headless/interactive managed team modes; mission read-state boundary. - Basis: explicit and structural - Confidence: medium-high for code-reachable mode; effective multi-agent performance not experimentally verified - Caveats: Ordinary single-agent ForceFlow remains S1-level work. MissionView only projects work state; it is not independently credited as S3.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Obtain independently sampled operational artifact evidence and a distinct reviewer judgment to challenge the completed VibeCode task outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The implementing agent may report success despite placeholder code, broken UX, missing tests, unsafe integration or package/build defects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separate read-only model reviewer examines actual project files and selects acceptance/failure findings; parsing enforces output shape, while the executing VibeCode loop returns its gaps as repair work. - Decision owner: Independent read-only reviewer Agent in first-party VibeCode completion mode; main coding agent acts on findings but does not author the audit verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runvibecodereview separate Agent with readonly=True/no history persistence, deterministic project-wide gate, typed pass/score/gaps format, parsevibecodereview and ForceFlow repair queue. - Closure path: Coding/verification receipts → separate reviewer instructed to inspect important files for missing implementation/integration/test evidence → independent structured gaps/pass → if failed append repair items and re-run implementation/quality loop; if accepted complete and persist report. - Boundary reachability: Shipped /vibe and /mission long paths invoke built-in runvibecodereview as final acceptance stage of the actual VibeCode loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer model generates source-informed judgment; deterministic JSON parsing and workqueue enforcement do not decide which defects exist. - Claim being audited: Main agent&#39;s completion/acceptance claim for the objective and implementation status. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementer tool/test receipts, task queue status, changed-file list and deterministic local gate. - Complementary access path: Separate read-only Agent with its own context directly inspects project files against the objective and catches defects not established by normal receipts. - Independence boundary: Reviewer is a new readonly=True Agent, recordhistory=False and no permission to edit; it receives objective/test/task evidence but must independently read actual project artifacts. - Who acts on findings: VibeCode current-control execution consumes gaps into repair queue, then root model implements fixes and repeats final review. - Evidence: runvibecodereview and VibeCode repair loop; VibeCode documented mode; receipt and repair gate. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party closed external-and-prospective organizational intelligence loop distinct from current coding/verification and manual provider setup is proven. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New provider capabilities, different project needs and tool ecosystem changes may matter; configurable providers and model routing do not show independently selected future adaptation programs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator chooses provider/model and package configuration; active coding agent can research the present task, not demonstrably decide future organizational capability in an S4→S3 relationship. - Decision owner: Human for config/product update, coder for current task; no distinct S4 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model selection, provider profiles, graph/repo intelligence, web lookup, skill loading, mission/vibe planning and snapshots. - Closure path: Config or task feedback → current model/operations; no outside-horizon scan → adaptation option → authority decision → returned changed team capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Current task planning, self-repair and model selection do not count as future/environment S4 without the required prospective coupling. - Evidence: provider model and Agent loop; configuration; model and extension controls. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime ultimate organizational purpose/constitutional policy adjudication loop with a returned whole-system mandate is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Safety sandbox, modellock, tool approval and read-only workers govern execution privilege, not the organization&#39;s ultimate identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator approves potentially risky actions and chooses provider/model, while deterministic sandbox enforces bounds; no distinct S5 identity owner. - Decision owner: Human for local trust and model choices, fixed runtime policy for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ForceSandbox, Smart Autopilot, modellock, worker role privilege, app config, redaction and workspace safety controls. - Closure path: Selected permission/model policy → restricted/approved tool; missing ultimate-policy issue → legitimate S5 decision → binding returned organization identity mandate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Enforcing a local safety rule or choosing a product role is not constitutive identity governance. - Evidence: sandbox; agent/fleet permission constraints; project security policy. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cfd322a4f8fe8eca7ab1b2275fed55ab7a28cd28/assessments/forcecode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cfd322a4f8fe8eca7ab1b2275fed55ab7a28cd28\">cfd322a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T20:02:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/W-Mai/atman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Atman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/W-Mai/atman/tree/56933fc40e38ccb5575cbfdda341d86a449643ac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 56933fc40e38ccb5575cbfdda341d86a449643ac\">56933fc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-directed code work within programmed .at agent flow, with tool/result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown files, failures in shell/test/edit commands, incomplete tasks and differing child work outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose native tools and code edits or next model goal in response to prior tool results, within typed flow permissions. - Decision owner: first-party managed Atman model-directed coding actor operating through llm.call and dispatchall; the .at script defines bounds rather than individual tool choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: managed AGENTAT, flow executor, llm.call, tool dispatch, session messages, native Git/fs/shell tools. - Closure path: user task→llm.call→model-selected tools→dispatchall executes→session.push feedback→subsequent model/tool choice or finished response. - Boundary reachability: atman init installs native managed flow; CLI/TUI default route and atman run can execute it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses concrete coding operations and adapts to observed tool results; full .at control-flow ownership is deterministic but does not eliminate adaptive S1. - Evidence: crates/atman-runtime/src/templates.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/executor.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/tools/llmcall.rs, README.md. - Basis: first-party supported native model loop and tool feedback. - Confidence: high for source-reachable operating path, no live task outcome test. - Caveats: CLI/DSL custom flows without model-choice tools can be deterministic rather than S1=A; current assessment is for shipped managed agent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: construct isolation that attenuates concrete shared-file write interference among separate coding child S1 actors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent child flows writing overlapping files in the same parent checkout. - Decisive decision or feedback right: allocate different Git worktrees for each child when workspace:auto/retain is requested, rejecting mismatched repository bindings and preserving separate result paths. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic workspace constructor (C) under operator/parent requested workspace policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: managed worktree service, per-child workspace id/lease, repository-root and owner validation, propagated child execution cwd and lifecycle finalization. - Closure path: parallel child spawn with workspace policy→FlowWorkspaceService.allocate creates child-specific Git worktree→child fs/bash/git tools use isolated working directory→distinct result files and lifecycle return avoid concurrent writes to parent&#39;s checkout. - Boundary reachability: native flow.spawn accepts workspace auto/retain in installed runtime, default none remains non-isolated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic first-party isolation changes the contested writable work domain, but model arbitration of semantic patch conflicts is not demonstrated. - Distinct S1 units: separate first-party subagent.at implementer or other child coding flows spawned from a parent session. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent editing of identical files or commands in the same repository checkout by multiple independent children. - Attenuating coordination relation: unique managed per-child worktrees and guarded workspace ownership isolate each writable Git checkout. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: accepted children execute in distinct roots; invalid bindings fail instead of permitting conflicting edits to parent root; child outputs return with workspace scope. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: explicit per-child writable workspace isolation attenuates inter-worker resource contention, not just sequencing or status transport. - Evidence: crates/atman-runtime/src/flowworkspace.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/gitworkspace.rs, README.md. - Basis: supported optional first-party collision attenuator. - Confidence: high for static worktree allocation, no concurrent live trial. - Caveats: worktree policy is opt-in; it does not resolve merge conflicts across resulting patches.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: model-owned whole-current management of ongoing child coding and verification flows in managed agent mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate tasks, stalled or irrelevant child work, tasks needing reassignment, overall progress and running/background child status. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect whole visible session&#39;s child population and decide which to spawn, reuse, kill, steer/interject or await, changing real execution commitments. - Decision owner: first-party managed primary model invoking flow.instances/status/output/spawn/kill/interject as separate child-control tools; registry owns safe execution of its decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session-scoped FlowInstances live inventory and one-use spawn token, model tool choices, native child registry, lifecycle/status and output, async watchers. - Closure path: current worker inventory and task outputs→primary model interprets whole group→kills duplicate work, spawns new work with token or redirects active child→tool execution mutates live child state→updated inventory/results feed back to parent. - Boundary reachability: templates.rs ships a managed agent with these exact exposed and recommended tools; first-party flow.instances and control actions are available when group exists, not hypothetical user-authored software. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model makes conditional whole-team current decisions from a live group view; unlike a fixed DSL stage list this changes commitments to address current work state. The same model can do S1 code work, but the child portfolio decision right is a differentiated metasystem function in the supported delegation mode. - Whole-system current view: session-scope inventory of all visible spawned child handles, goal, status, model, run id, output and watchers, plus parent goal and prior task results. - Current-control decision scope: choose child assignment and active/cancelled commitments, reuse current work, redirect or terminate a worker rather than merely issue own file actions. - Evidence: crates/atman-runtime/src/templates.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/tools/flowlist.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/tools/agentctrl.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/taskregistry.rs. - Basis: built-in autonomous primary supervisor over subordinate first-party flows. - Confidence: medium-high for supported path, dynamic decisions not live witnessed. - Caveats: without spawned children, the ordinary agent loop does not expose a whole-team portfolio to regulate.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge candidate completion using a distinct model-backed disposition judge and, in explicit verification mode, an independently tasked read/test child whose findings can drive correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: premature final.answer, candidate summaries omitting required work, unsupported completion claims and untested code modifications. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge completion relative to original request and recent transcript, trigger continueaction/continuework instead of returning; in verify-child mode separately select file/read/test evidence actions and report independent findings. - Decision owner: first-party separate llm.classify model invocation at managed agent handback, and optional first-party verify-role model actor on distinct child flow; parent receives findings for corrective coding. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTAT managed final-answer disposition schema, recent-turn evidence, answer origin, injected continuation directives, first-party SUBAGENTAT verifyloop with restricted tools and separate session. - Closure path: producer candidate final response→separate llm.classify checks request/transcript and emits disposition→negative continuation gate appends correction to live parent agent; optional verify child reads/tests repo directly→findings returned via flow output→parent implementation/decision. - Boundary reachability: installed atman init managed agent automatically calls disposition judge for no-tool final candidates; dedicated verify-role subagent is shipped and model-callable but optional. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a distinct model invocation/child role, not the producer&#39;s self-claim, chooses whether reported work is sufficient; the first-party flow enforces re-entry. It does not automatically verify all code with a test suite. - Claim being audited: user-request completion for a proposed final answer; optionally correctness of implementation as observed by dedicated verify child. - Ordinary reporting path: producer model response with final.answer, tool transcript and running child work. - Complementary access path: classifier gets independent memory.recentturns evidence and original request, separate from candidate text; optional verify child can read source/Git diff and run tests in its own model/tool loop. - Independence boundary: completion classifier is a separate model call with a role-specific category contract, not a producer statement; verify role is a separate first-party child run with read/test rights. Neither implies externally attested reviewer independence. - Who acts on findings: managed flow directly blocks premature exit by injecting a continuation directive, and parent agent consumes child verification findings and may repair work before a later answer. - Evidence: crates/atman-runtime/src/templates.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/tools/llmclassify.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/tools/finalanswer.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/tools/agentctrl.rs. - Basis: source-confirmed separate model judgment and binding narrow completion return; optional read/test evidence mode. - Confidence: medium-high for configured first-party pathways. - Caveats: classifier&#39;s evidence is a recent transcript excerpt, not an independent workspace check; stronger code-claim audit depends on explicitly spawning verify role. Absence of an observed live model decision is not hidden.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external/future intelligence actor selecting and rolling out durable new capabilities from environmental evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task failures, stored confessions, rule changes and changing context are addressed through ordinary memory and coding control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: detect outside/future change and decide a prospective installed capability adaptation with binding return into new operations; not established. - Decision owner: developer/operator can edit .at flows or memory manually; no first-party prospective strategy-selection organ demonstrated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory.confess, rules, history, project goals, flow authoring and tool availability. - Closure path: durable confessions inject corrective hints into current/future coding, but do not autonomously choose new tools/flows or govern future systemic capacity under a distinct owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: remembering errors and allowing DSL editing are useful but not a genuine outside-and-then option→implementation cycle. - Evidence: crates/atman-runtime/src/memory/confession.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/templates.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/flowregistry.rs. - Basis: bounded negative first-party source inspection. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: user can construct S4 using external flow programs, which are not the credited standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate identity/purpose interpretation organ with independent legitimate authority beyond user-defined task and runtime security policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: untrusted flows, unsupported permissions, scope changes and spec design uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adjudicate governing system identity and ultimate organizational purpose with binding decisions for S3/S4; not established. - Decision owner: legitimate user authorizes specific spec designs, sandbox capabilities and commands; DSL enforces these bounds but does not independently decide organizational constitutional policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static flow contract.scope checking, capability sandbox, human form.ask with revision-bound approval receipts, TUI permissions and role prompts. - Closure path: spec confirmation grants/denies exact design, tool permission allows or denies a call; neither establishes a rightful ultimate identity owner governing system ends. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: narrow task approvals and exact-revision authorization are operating governance, not distinct S5 identity/purpose self-rule. - Evidence: crates/atman-runtime/src/approval.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/permission.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/templates.rs. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an adopting organization can supply external S5 policy and custom flows, outside this published harness&#39;s recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ec3da6b24a3c0f24211b112c75b66db92476a90a/assessments/atman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ec3da6b24a3c0f24211b112c75b66db92476a90a\">ec3da6b &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
